## 1. INTRODUCTION
Hydrogen sulfide $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}S\right)$ is a colorless gas with a characteristic odor of rotten eggs. It is highly toxic, corrosive, and flammable in nature. Since $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is heavier than air, it has a tendency to settle in low-lying and poorly ventilated areas (see Table 1 for the properties of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ ). It causes irritation to the eyes, nose, and throat at concentrations as low as ${\mathfrak{s}}{\mathfrak{p p m}}$ and is fatal over 1000 ppm.1 Inhalation is the primary route for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ exposure. At ${\sim}30$ ppm, $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ paralyzes the sense of smell and is rapidly absorbed by the blood, resulting in poor oxygen uptake at the cellular level. Therefore, one should not fully trust their sense of smell for the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ because alarming concentrations may be present with no perceivable odor. Handling of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ requires stringent safety regulations such as trained personnel, complete set of personal protective equipment including a gas mask, strategically located sensors, and emergency evacuation plans. Experimental studies with $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ at concentrations of a few hundred parts per million are challenging enough; the challenge is even greater at the percentage-level concentrations in actual gas-processing facilities.
$\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ occurs naturally, at varied concentrations, in various hydrocarbon sources such as crude petroleum, natural gas, and biogas (see Table 2). It is also formed by the hydrolysis reaction of metal sulfides present in volcanic rocks. Anaerobic respiration of sulfate-reducing bacteria, whereby these organisms breathe sulfate to derive energy and release sulfide as waste, constitutes another major natural source of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ . Landfills, rotten vegetation, and composting sites can also contribute to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ generation; caution should be exercised in dealing with these not-so-obvious sources of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ .
Anthropogenically, the main source of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is in oil refineries, wherein desulfurization of various gas streams using hydrogen gas generates $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ (also known as hydrodesulfurization). This process is essential to reduce the sulfur content of automobile and heating fuels, since combustion of sulfur compounds will result in emission of harmful sulfur oxides to the atmosphere that can, in turn, give rise to acid rain. Hydrodesulfurization is also important since sulfur compounds, often at very low concentrations, can deactivate noble metal catalysts for downstream catalytic reforming units. Gasification of coal and biomass for syngas $\mathrm{~(CO~+~H}_{2})$ production, under high temperature and low oxygen concentrations, results in a conversion of the sulfur content of these resources into $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ .
For any of the hydrocarbon streams, such as natural gas, biogas, or syngas, to be useful for energy generation or chemicals production, they need to be purified to remove unacceptable amounts of acid gases such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The acceptable $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ levels can be quite different depending on the end use; for example, ${5{\mathrm{-}}15\ \mathrm{ppm\H}_{2}S}$ is acceptable in natural gas depending on the country of use, but $<1\mathrm{\ppm\H}_{2}S$ is required for fuel cell applications, where the electrodes are very susceptible to sulfur poisoning. The magnitudes of different streams that are processed globally are also quite different: for instance, over 120 trillion standard cubic feet (scf) of natural gas is produced each year, while only about 1 trillion scf of biogas is produced annually. The optimal materials and processes for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal from different sources and for different end applications can be quite different. In this Review, we will focus more on the technical aspects of the different alternative methods and processes. Detailed economic analysis of competing $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ -removal processes for specific feeds and applications is beyond the scope of this Review. While there is a significant overlap in materials for different $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ -capture problems, we will base our discussions mainly in the context of natural gas purification.
Raw natural gas is a complex mixture of methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ , ethane, and other light alkanes, $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ carbon dioxide $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ , nitrogen, and water $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)$ vapor. The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency classifies natural gas as sour at $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentrations above $4\ \mathrm{ppm};^{5}$ sweetening refers to removal of acidic sulfur compounds, primarily $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ . Natural gas emerging at the reservoir well head is subjected to low temperatures to condense out heavier hydrocarbons (see Figure 1). Overhead vapors are sent to the acid gas removal unit to selectively strip off $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2};$ amine-based absorption is most commonly used for this step. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ -rich stream is sent to the sulfur recovery unit (SRU), while the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ -rich stream, after some post-processing steps such as dehydration, is sent to the pipeline as sales gas. In the SRU, sulfur is recovered by the wellknown Claus process, where $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ undergoes high-temperature $({\sim}1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ thermal oxidation,
$$
\mathrm{H}_{2}S+1.5\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mathrm{SO}_{2}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
$$
-\Delta H=518{-}576\mathrm{kJ/mol}\mathrm{of}\mathrm{H}_{2}S
$$
prior to low-temperature $(200-300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ catalytic oxidation,
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}+\mathrm{SO}_{2}\rightleftharpoons3\mathrm{S}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}\\ &{-\Delta H=88-146\mathrm{kJ}/\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{of}\mathrm{SO}_{2}}\end{array}
$$
( $-\Delta H$ data from ref 6), in a series of reactors at progressively lower temperatures, accompanied by sulfur removal in intermediate condensers. Since the melting point of elemental sulfur is $115^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and sulfur deposition leads to catalyst deactivation, the temperature of the final Claus reactor is generally maintained above $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This results in an incomplete conversion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{;}}$ , and the resulting gas stream is sent to the tail gas treatment unit for additional sulfur recovery before the waste gases are released to the atmosphere. Thus, highly sour natural gas is usually processed for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal in two different units, at two very different concentrations.
In addition to other papers in this special issue, Chemical Reviews has published three papers on the subject of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture in the past 5 years: Sumida et al. reviewed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture in metal−organic frameworks (MOFs),7 Pera-Titus reviewed the capture process using porous inorganic membranes,8 and Sanz-Pérez et al. focused on chemical sorbents for the direct capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from air.9 However, similar reviews on $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capture are few and limited in scop e.10−16 Abatzoglou and Boivin reviewed biogas purification processes for removal of contaminants such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{;}}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ and siloxanes.11 They divided the purification methods into two generic categories: those involving physicochemical phenomena and those involving biological processes. Most biotechnologies still require further optimization and scale-up in order to compete with their physical/chemical counterparts.17 We will focus here on the physicochemical techniques for selective $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal; the reader interested in microbiological alternatives is referred to earlier reviews on the subject.18,19 The abundant literature available on $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal may be broadly classified into four categories: absorption, adsorption, membranes, and cryogenic distillation (see Figure 2). In this Review, we focus on materials and chemistries involved in each of these processes. Extensive review articles focus on solutions to tackle concentrated $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ by methods like the Claus process20−22 or the production of hydrogen from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S},^{23}$ and these are not considered here.
Since $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is a polar molecule, while $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is a nonpolar molecule, it is obvious to imagine a polar solvent or a polar adsorbent surface to carry out this separation. However, the presence of other polar natural gas impurities such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ which has a dipole moment about twice that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ , presents challenges to a simple polarity-based $\mathrm{CH_{4}/H_{2}S}$ separation. Exploiting the higher acidity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ compared to that of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ using basic chemicals such as amines is also an alternative for sour gas sweetening. However, this principle of separation also suffers from some limitations due to the acidity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ being higher than that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and the concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in natural gas being generally much higher than that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ . The kinetic diameter of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $\left(3.8\mathrm{~\AA~}\right)$ is slightly larger than that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ (3.6 Å); this might be another property that can be exploited for this particular gas separation using nanoporous adsorbents or membranes. Thus, while several possible solutions exist, challenges are associated with each of them, and it is important to evaluate these on a case-by-case basis.
## 2. ABSORPTION
The natural gas industry has relied on gas absorption for purification of raw natural gas for nearly 100 years, and this separation technique dominates most sour gas separations around the globe even today. A variety of different liquid solvents have been employed in a tray or a packed-bed configuration to selectively absorb $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and light alkanes. Depending on the strength of interaction of gas molecules with the solvent and the nature of the absorption isotherm, these solvents maybe broadly classified into chemical and physical categories. For chemisorption, solubility of the gas in the liquid increases sharply at very low pressures and starts to plateau at higher pressures; the plateau is indicative of the stoichiometric capacity for the chemical reaction. In physisorption, the plateau is shifted to much higher pressures, and in many cases there is no plateau for the pressure range of interest, so solubility continues to increase, more or less linearly, with pressure. A sharp distinction between these categories does not exist (e.g., hydrogen bonding and weak charge transfer), and in reality, all solute−solvent combinations have some contribution from physisorption. For sour gas sweetening, alkanolamines are the most widely used class of chemical solvents. All other solvents usually absorb the gas physically, and their very high capacities make them of interest if a feed with high acid gas partial pressure is available.
## 2.1. Alkanolamines
Bottoms for the first time, in 1930, developed an absorption process for acid gases using alkanolamine solutions.24 For several years, diethanolamine (DEA) was commonly used in refineries to remove acid gases. However, not only do $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentrations in raw natural gas vary over a wide range of concentrations, but also since the toxicity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is much higher than that of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ the upper-bound specifications for these impurities in the pipeline gas are also quite different (4 ppm for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $2\%$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ). Clearly, the extent of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ removal required can be very different too. For example, nearly complete removal of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ without any major removal of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is desired for a feed gas containing high $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentration but $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration being already at $2\%$ . Traditional alkanolamines absorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in preference to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ ; Frazier and Kohl first proposed the use of tertiary amines such as methyldiethanolamine (MDEA) for selective removal of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ .25 The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}-$ tertiary amine reaction,
$$
\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\mathrm{R}_{3}\mathrm{N}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{R}_{3}\mathrm{NH}^{+}+\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}
$$
proceeding via the bicarbonate formation, is relatively slow compared with the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ −primary and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ −secondary amine reactions, resulting in a carbamate ion,
$$
\mathrm{CO}_{2}+2\mathrm{R}_{2}\mathrm{NH}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{R}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}+\mathrm{R}_{2}\mathrm{NCOO}^{-}
$$
while the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S\cdot$ −tertiary amine reaction, like all other amines, involves a proton transfer and is faster,26
$$
\mathrm{H}_{2}S+\mathrm{R}_{3}\mathrm{N}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{R}_{3}\mathrm{NH}^{+}+\mathrm{H}S^{-}
$$
MDEA not only offers kinetic selectivity toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ but also allows an equilibrium that favors the absorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ over CO2.25,27
Besides MDEA, diisopropanolamine (DIPA), which is used in the Adip process developed by Shell, has also been reported to have a greater selectivity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ over $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ than either monoethanolamine (MEA) or DEA.28 In 1983, Sartori et al. investigated 2-amino-2-methyl-1-propanol (AMP), a sterically hindered amine, for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ removal at Exxon Research and Engineering Company.29 Introducing steric hindrance by a bulky substituent adjacent to the amino group lowers the stability of the carbamate formed by the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ −amine reaction. Using kinetic modeling, the authors described as to how decreased carbamate stability leads to higher thermodynamic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ loadings approaching $\mathrm{~1~mol~}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per mole of amine. Additionally, this leads to a higher free-amine concentration; therefore fast $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ −amine reaction rates are obtained despite some reduction of the rate constant owing to steric interference. These hindered amines were found to be selective toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ over $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and have been commercialized as the Flexsorb process by Exxon Research and Engineering Company. It is claimed that the new hindered amine-based processes are potentially attractive replacements for the existing selective $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal processes, including the MDEA-based process.28
Thorough knowledge of the vapor−liquid equilibria for a gas/liquid combination is a prerequisite for accurate design of absorption columns. Classical thermodynamic modeling of acid gas absorption in aqueous alkanolamines, using either semiempirical equilibrium constant approaches or activity coefficient methods, has been a subject of interest since the 1930s. Recently, Suleman et al. reviewed these models, discussing their classification, chronological development, salient features, and recommendations of appropriate models depending on the pressure and loading of the acid gases.30
## 2.2. Methanol
The Rectisol process, developed independently by Linde and Lurgi in the late 1950s, involves physical absorption using methanol at temperatures between $-40$ and $\ensuremath{-60}~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Since methanol has a low viscosity, it can be used for very lowtemperature absorption processes, in particular for feeds with low partial pressures of acid gases. Since the high vapor pressure of methanol demands washing of all streams with liquid water in order to recover the methanol vapors, all streams are humidified during the process; the purified gas stream will also be humidified, thus requiring an additional step to meet the upper-bound $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ specifications for natural gas. The main application for the Rectisol process is purification of syngas derived from the gasification of heavy oil and coal rather than natural gas treating applications.31 The multi-stage Ifpexol process, developed by Institut Francais du Petrole, can be used for natural gas applications.32 Physical properties of various commercially used physical solvents for gas sweetening are listed in Table 3.33,34
## 2.3. N-Methyl-2-pyrrolidone
Like the Rectisol process, the Purisol process is also a physical absorption process based on $N.$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP). However, due to its much lower vapor pressure, it can be operated at relatively higher temperatures without excessive solvent loss. Its operation at sub-ambient temperatures allows for simultaneous water removal since water is not required to wash the treated gas stream for solvent (NMP) recovery. Various advantages and limitations of the Rectisol and Purisol processes are covered in great detail elsewhere.34,35
## 2.4. Poly(ethylene glycol) Dimethyl Ether
Poly(ethylene glycol) dimethyl ether (PEGDME) is a mixture of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{C}\bar{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{n}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{H}_{3}$ oligomers with $\ n\ =\ 2{-}9$ . Compared to the other solvents, PEGDME has a higher viscosity, which reduces mass-transfer rates and tray efficiencies and increases packing or tray requirements, especially at reduced temperatures.34 However, it benefits from lower solvent vapor pressure, which prevents solvent losses in various streams, thus eliminating the need for water wash to recover the solvent and allowing for simultaneous dehydration of the feed to the desired dew point of sales gas. The Selexol process, developed by Allied Chemical Corporation, was first utilized in 1970.
## 2.5. Sulfolane and Diisopropanolamine
Unlike other physical solvents such as methanol, sulfolane are rarely used alone but rather in admixture with a chemical solvent such as an alkanolamine solution.36 Shell Oil’s Sulfinol process, first developed by Dunn et al. in 1964, employs a mixed solvent consisting of DIPA, sulfolane, and water.37 The presence of the physical solvent appreciably enhances the solution capacity, especially when the gas stream to be treated is available at high pressure and the acidic components are present in high concentrations.38 There have been several subsequent studies to improve the chemical solvent; one such candidate is MDEA, a tertiary amine that has thermodynamic and kinetic selectivity toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in the presence of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .39−41 Currently, three different solvents are available for the Sulfinol process: Sulfinol-X consists of MDEA and piperazine, SulfinolM consists of MDEA, and Sulfinol-D consists of DIPA.42
## 2.6. Ionic Liquids
Ionic liquids (ILs) are salts having moderately low melting points $(\bar{<}100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , with their ionic character imparting them a large cohesive energy in the liquid phase, which in turn results in extremely low vapor pressures over a wide temperature range. This is a benefit for absorptive gas separation since the loss of solvent to the gas stream can be minimized. Additionally, many ILs are thermally very stable and are less combustible than other absorbents. Typically, an IL comprises a large, low-symmetry organic cation derived by alkyl substitution on parent cations such as imidazolium, pyridinium, ammonium, and phosphonium and an inorganic or organic anion such as tetrafluoroborate, hexafluorophosphate, acetate, methyl sulfate, ethyl sulfate, triflate, etc.43 Due to their polar character, ILs show a high affinity for polar molecules such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ while nonpolar molecules like $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and other alkanes exhibit a much smaller solubility. In this section, we will discuss various advantages and shortcomings of ILs for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capture. The reader is also referred to earlier reviews on the subject.44−46
Jou and Mather first investigated $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ absorption in an IL, 1- $N$ -butyl-3-methylimidazolium hexafluorophosphate $\left[\mathrm{b}\mathrm{mim}^{+}\right].$ - $\lbrack\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}\rbrack)$ , and observed that $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ only physisorbed to the IL.47 This further suggests that ILs are unlikely to replace alkanolamines completely but may be useful either for the bulk removal of high-partial-pressure acid gases or for mixing with a chemical solvent to increase capacity. Pomelli et al. extended this investigation to a wide range of cations and anions and demonstrated ILs with extremely high $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ solubilities.62 They also carried out gas-phase electronic structure calculations elucidating the strong binding of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ to the anion of the ${\mathrm{IL}},$ with interaction energies of these complexes in the range of $30{-}60\ \mathrm{kJ/mol},$ , indicating traditional hydrogen bonds. Experimental data for the Henry’s law constants for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{.}}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in various ILs are summarized in Table 4.
Since sorption experiments are much more difficult to conduct for gas mixtures than for pure gases, an assumption needs to be invoked for defining absorption selectivity. There are at least two possible ways to define ideal absorption selectivity that have been used in the literature: (i) ratio of the solubilities at infinite dilution (Henry’s law coefficients) and (ii) computing a mixture selectivity,
$$
S_{i j}=\frac{x_{i}/x_{j}}{y_{i}/y_{j}}
$$
assuming that the amount absorbed for a mixture at a given partial pressure is equal to that for a pure case at the same total pressure. The answers obtained from the two definitions can be quite different. In order to assess the validity of ideal absorption selectivity approximation, Singh et al.63 carried out molecular simulations for mixture absorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in $\left[\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{mim}^{+}\right]$ - $\left[\mathrm{Tf}_{2}\mathbf{N}^{-}\right]$ and $[\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{mim}^{+}][\mathrm{Tf}_{2}\mathrm{N}^{-}]$ and found that definition (ii) for selectivity is in much better agreement with mixture selectivity. For $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ absorption in $[\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{mim}^{+}][\mathrm{Tf}_{2}\mathrm{N}^{-}],$ Budhathoki et al. observed definition (i) to be in better agreement with the mixture selectivity.64 These differences demonstrate a lack of quantitative predictive capabilities to determine mixture absorption from pure-component data and suggest that either mixture measurements or mixture simulations with reliable force fields should indeed be carried out for at least the more promising ILs.
Trends in affinity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ toward the anion of the IL were found to depend on the cation under consideration: $\left[\mathrm{emim}^{+}\right]$ - $\big[\mathrm{Tf}_{2}\mathrm{N^{-}}\big]~>~\mathsf{\bar{\Gamma}}\big[\mathrm{emim^{+}}\big]\big[\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}\big],^{5+}$ 6 $\mathrm{[bmim^{+}][T f_{2}N^{-}]~>~[b m i m^{+}]}$ - $\mathrm{\big[BF_{4}^{-}\big]>\big[b m i m^{+}\big]\big[P F_{6}^{-}\big],^{49}\big[h m i m^{+}\big]\big[B F_{4}^{-}}$ $\mathrm{PF_{6}^{~-}\big]_{,}^{49}~[h m i m^{+}]\big[B F_{4}^{~-}\big]>~[h m i m^{+}]\big[P F_{6}^{~-}\big]}$ $\approx\mathrm{[hmim^{+}]}\mathrm{[Tf_{2}N^{-}]},\AA^{52}$ and $\mathrm{[hemim^{+}]}\mathrm{[Tf_{2}N^{-}]}\ \geq\ \mathrm{[hemim^{+}]}$ - $\mathrm{\small{[OTf^{-}]}>\[h e m i m^{+}][P F_{6}^{-}]>[h e m i m^{+}][B F_{4}^{-}]}$ .54 These results indicate that $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ solubility is affected by both the cation and the anion of the ${\mathrm{IL}},$ and it may not be possible to find the best anion and the best cation without trying all possible pairs. With the aid of molecular simulations, deeper insights can be gained about the entropic and enthalpic contributions of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ solubilities in different ILs. Recently, Sánchez-Badillo and coworkers computed solvation thermodynamics of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in ILs and demonstrated qualitative agreement with experimental data.65 These calculations showed that, at room temperature, the absolute solvation enthalpy is 2 times higher than the absolute entropic contribution for $\mathbf{\bar{[bmim^{+}]}[P F_{6}^{\mathbf{-}}]}$ and $[\mathrm{bmim^{+}}][\mathrm{BF_{4}^{\ -}}]$ and 3 times higher for $[\mathrm{bmim^{+}}][\mathrm{Cl^{-}}]$ . The solubility of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in $[\mathrm{bmim^{+}}][\mathrm{Tf_{2}N^{-}}]$ from molecular simulations was shown to be sensitive to the choice of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ force field; the TraPPE model resulted in the most accurate predictions.66,67 Jalili et al. also studied $[\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{mim}^{+}][\mathrm{Tf}_{2}\mathrm{N}^{-}]$ and found that the solubility of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in $\left[\mathsf{C}_{n}\mathrm{mim}^{+}\right]\left[\mathrm{Tf}_{2}\mathrm{N}^{-}\right]$ ILs increases as the number of carbon atoms in the alkyl substituent of the methylimidazolium cation, $n_{\mathrm{{;}}}$ , increases.58 The authors explained that since the molar volume of the $\mathrm{IL}$ increases with $n_{\mathrm{{\ell}}}$ , possibly due to poorer interactions between bulkier cations and the anion, higher free volumes may result, in turn increasing the solubility. One must remember that such increased acid gas solubility due to increased free volumes will also lead to increased $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ solubility, in turn reducing $S_{\mathrm{H}_{2}S/C H_{4}}$ . As described previously with regard to how the necessity for selective $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ over $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ absorption led to the development of absorbents such as MDEA, new solvents with $S_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}}>1$ that also exert a low vapor pressure to reduce solvent losses will be interesting targets, and ILs, with their tunable polarity, can be groundbreaking candidates to achieve this selectivity.68 Investigations of ternary systems consisting of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $\left[\mathrm{C_{8}}\mathrm{\bar{mim}^{+}}\right]$ - $\left[\mathrm{Tf}_{2}\mathbf{N}^{-}\right]$ showed $S_{\mathrm{H}_{2}S/C O_{2}}\approx3\$ under ambient pressure and temperature.58
All the studies conducted so far focused only on $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and not on $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ absorption. After all, for gas sweetening, selectivity is more important than solubility alone. Carvalho et al. suggested that the solubility of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in ILs is governed predominantly by entropic effects and that the most promising approach to enhance the sour gases/ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivities at high pressures is by lowering the methane solubility in ILs and not by increasing the sour gas solubility, which is essentially dependent on the solvent molar volume.69 These authors measured $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ solubility in four different ILs and demonstrated that it was highly dependent on the polarity of the $\mathrm{IL}$ and that, contrary to the previous selectivity models in the literature,70,71 the molar volume of the IL alone was not a sufficient criterion to tune selectivity.72
While high solubility and selectivity for the desired molecule form the prerequisites for an IL to be useful for separation purposes, additional criteria such as thermal and mass transport through the IL can also contribute to the overall operating cost of the absorber. Due to their high cohesive energies, ILs demonstrate high viscosities, 1−3 orders of magnitude higher than for the conventional solvents.73 Higher viscosities imply higher power consumption for gas−liquid contact, but also an unusual possibility for diffusion-based selectivity in a gas−liquid system. Under such circumstances, it will be helpful if the diffusivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is higher than that of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in order to benefit from a transport selectivity. Reports investigating the diffusion of acid gases in ILs are limited.55,57,74 At room temperature, [emim+][EtSO4−] shows a high diffusion selectivity, +H S/CO2 $\approx40$ while $[\mathrm{hemim^{+}}][\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}]$ shows $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{H}_{2}S/\mathrm{CO}_{2}}\approx1$ .
In 2002, Bates et al. first reported an IL designed for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture (task-specific ionic liquids (TSILs)) where a primary amine moiety is covalently tethered to an imidazolium cation for enhanced interaction with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .75 Huang et al. employed carboxylate-based ILs for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capture, the Lewis base character of which resulted in higher absorption capacities.59 These ILs absorb about $0.3\mathrm{\mol}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ per mole of $\mathrm{IL}$ under ambient pressure at $60\ {}^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}},$ about 15 times higher than conventional ILs. The enthalpy of this reactive absorption varies between 30 and $65~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , depending on the cation and the anion, but is still lower than typical enthalpies of absorption in alkanolamines $\left(\geq80\mathrm{~kJ/mol}\right)$ . However, in spite of these favorable characteristics, the $S_{\mathrm{H}_{2}S/C O_{2}}$ in the carboxylate ILs is even worse than in the normal ILs due to the strong affinity of carboxylate ions toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .76 This challenge was overcome with the design of dual Lewis base functionalized ILs (DLB-ILs),77 which have a tertiary amine group tethered to the carboxylate anion. It was found that the DLB-ILs could reversibly absorb $0.39-0.84~\mathrm{mol}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ per mole of $\mathrm{IL}$ at 1 bar and $60\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ which is significantly higher than for other ILs. These DLB-ILs show $S_{\mathrm{H}_{2}S/C O_{2}}\approx20$ at 1 bar and $S_{\mathrm{H}_{2}S/C O_{2}}>100$ at pressures less than 0.1 bar. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations demonstrated the favorable geometry and energetics of the carboxylate $\left.-\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}-\right.$ amine complex and the reduced ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{-IL}}$ binding.
Although TSILs can be modified for high $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capacity and $S_{\mathrm{H}_{2}S/\mathrm{CO}_{2}},$ their high costs and viscosity disfavor their application in industry.60 This limitation encouraged the same authors to explore protic ionic liquid (PILs), which are a class of low-cost ILs that can be easily prepared from simple materials through direct neutralization between the corresponding acid and base.60,78 For instance, mixing dimethylethanolamine and acetic acid yields dimethylethanolammonium acetate ([DMEAH]- $[\mathrm{Ac}])$ ). It was demonstrated that $\mathrm{\big[DMEAH\big]}[\mathrm{Ac}]$ and dimethylethanolammonium formate ([DMEAH][For]) have SH2S/CO2 greater than those of normal ILs and any other organic solvent.
Another issue with TSILs is that they are highly hydrophilic and miscible with water, thus increasing the expense of regeneration by requiring energy for removing bound water. Both the cation and anion can affect the mutual solubilities between water and ILs, but the anion plays the major role in determining their phase behavior.79 Huang et al.61 synthesized half-protonated diamines with a free tertiary amine group (disfavoring $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ ) tethered on the cations and bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl)imide $\big(\mathrm{[Tf_{2}N^{-}]}\big)$ as the anion, which is a wellknown building block for hydrophobic ILs with low viscosity.79 Although these ILs have $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ absorption capacity similar to that of other TSILs reported in the literature on a mole ratio basis, they have a molecular weight nearly twice that of other TSILs, making capacity on molality basis (per mass of IL) nearly half. As described earlier, compared to common organic solvents with high vapor pressures, ILs contribute negligible volatile organic compounds to the atmosphere, thus reducing the issue of atmospheric emissions. However, it has been noted in the literature that most ILs are not intrinsically “green”,80 and similar to other commercial organic solvents, irresponsible release of them will pose a threat to the environment. One of the key issues here is that, unlike the common solvents, the toxicity of ILs is not fully characterized. However, at least in principle, the large design space of ILs should allow for designing solvents that not only are effective for the application of interest but also pose no serious ecological threat. In an attempt to explore environmentally benign solvents, Guo et al. synthesized and measured $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ absorption in a series of caprolactam tetrabutylammonium bromide (CPL−TBAB) ILs with different mole ratios of CPL and TBAB.81 For the 1:1 CPL−TBAB mixture, the solubilities of pure $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ at atmospheric pressure are 0.69 and $0.44~\mathrm{mol}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ per mole of $\mathrm{IL}$ at 30 and $90\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively.
Heldebrant et al. developed a new class of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -binding organic liquids $\left(\mathbf{CO_{2}B O L s}\right)$ that chemically and reversibly absorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .82 These organic liquids are mixtures of organic alcohols and amidine/guanidine bases. Since these organic liquids have lower specific heats compared to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and also since they require much lower regeneration temperatures, a higher energy efficiency can be achieved during absorption/ regeneration cycles. Following on the capture of acid gases such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ ${\mathrm{SO}}_{2},$ $\mathrm{CS}_{2},$ and COS using such organic liquids,82,83 it was hypothesized that weak bases such as tertiary alkanolamines (anhydrous) could react with $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ to form liquid hydrosulfide salts that could be used as reversible $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ -binding organic liquids $\left(\mathrm{{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SBOLs}}\right)$ .84 These authors demonstrated desorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ by either inert gas sparging or mild heating, or even at room temperature by the addition of low-specificheat antisolvents such as hexanes that can be boiled at $50{-}70\$ $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . BOLs are non-ionic before reacting with the gas molecule; however, after chemically reacting with the gas, they become ILs.
Very similar in properties to ILs, deep eutectic solvents (DESs) are a relatively new class of solvents that are formed by complexing a large, asymmetric quaternary ammonium salt with a metal salt or a hydrogen bond donor.85 Hydrogen bonding in the system results in charge delocalization, thus lowering the lattice energy and decreasing the melting point of the mixture relative to the melting points of the individual components.86 In addition to most of the better solvent properties of ILs, DESs are generally more biodegradable, less toxic, and inexpensive, thus making these solvents more sustainable. Articles on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture using DESs have started to appear in the past few years,87,88 and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ removal from natural gas was reviewed recently.89 To the best of our knowledge, there is no report in the literature on $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capture, and this might be an unexplored opportunity to discover novel solvents for this application.
Since 2010, several research groups have started to focus on equations of state (EoS) modeling of $\mathrm{~H}_{2}S^{\mathrm{~\tiny~+~}}$ ILs phase behavior.90−98 A major drawback in the use of the most prevalent cubic EoS is that they require critical parameters of the ILs that can only be obtained indirectly and with large uncertainties, limiting their utility to correlation purposes only.99 In contrast, contributions from various energetic interactions, such as dipole−dipole, quadrupole−quadrupole, and dipole−quadrupole, can be explicitly included in a class of equations based on the statistical associating fluid theory (SAFT) formalism.90,92 Several other correlation techniques that use molecular weight, critical temperature and pressure, and acentric factor as input variables have been applied to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ absorption in ILs.100−103 G roup contribution methods, requiring only the number and type of each fragment type as the input, were used to establish a comprehensive database on the solubility of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in ILs using 1334 pieces of data covering the period from 2007 to 2016.104
Lei et al. employed the conductor-like screening model for real solvents (COSMO-RS) to predict the solubilities of different gases, including $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ in ILs and observed deviations of less than $40\%$ .43 This approach requires only the molecular structure and is independent of any experimental correlation. In another study, Mortazavi-Manesh et al. used COSMO-RS to screen 425 ILs for sour gas separations and, based on binary selectivities, suggested 58 promising ILs with ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}S/{\mathrm{CH}}_{4},$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S/$ ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6},$ and ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}/{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ selectivities in the ranges 51−75, 11−23, and 2.6−3.4, respectively.
In 1977, Tennyson and Schaaf provided some guidelines for the choice of appropriate physical/chemical solvent depending on the partial pressures of acid gases in the feed and product gas streams.106 Process optimization for absorption using ILs in comparison to the commercial solvents can help identify the partial pressure regimes under which certain ILs may be preferred over conventional solvents. The past decade has seen several reports demonstrating high $_{\mathrm{H_{2}S-I L}}$ affinities, but ILs for selective acid gas removal are still in the design stage. Several design variables, such as length of the alkyl chains on the cation, branching of the alkyl chain, task-specific functional groups, pairing with appropriate anions, etc., make this a multidimensional combinatorial problem. As is shown in several fundamental studies for discovery of novel solid adsorbents, insights from molecular simulations can potentially speed up discovery of optimal ILs and DESs for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capture. Additionally, simulations can also help understand the differences between selectivities calculated from pure-component absorption isotherms and those observed in an actual mixture measurement. In conclusion, while ILs enjoy clear advantages such as design flexibility and negligible vapor pressures, properties such as viscosity, diffusivity, thermal conductivity, and reaction kinetics in ILs under varying temperatures and partial pressures of acid gases require thorough investigations in order to compare these new solvents with the conventional solvents that have been successfully commercialized for several decades.
## 3. ADSORPTION
Adsorptive separation involves the use of a selective and highcapacity solid substrate that shows a preferential affinity for one or more molecule types from their mixtures. Similar to absorption, adsorption may also be classified as physisorption and chemisorption, depending on the strength of sorbate− sorbent interactions. In addition to interaction-based separation, different sizes and shapes of molecules can also be exploited for selective removal of one compound over another. In this section, we will discuss a variety of adsorbents, such as metal oxides, MOFs, zeolites, carbons, and composite materials, for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capture from gas mixtures containing $\mathrm{CH}_{4},\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{~N~}$ ${\bf N}_{2},$ $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ and/or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . For any adsorption application, it is desirable that the adsorbent has a high separation selectivity for the molecule of interest (primarily $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in this case) and also a high capacity for the adsorbate. In addition to capacity and selectivity, stability under extreme acid gas environments as well as performance and structural regenerability are important for longer lifetime of the adsorbent bed.
## 3.1. Metal Oxides
Since the 1960s, metal oxides have been used for desulfurization of coal gas, syngas, hydrogen for fuel cells, and Claus tail gas.107,108 Metal oxides for hot-gas desulfurization have been investigated extensively;109,110 this large body of literature is beyond the scope of this Review, and we focus here mostly on low to moderate temperatures $(25-300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ that are of relevance to natural gas sweetening, including the Claus tail gas. In 1976, Westmoreland and Harrison screened for the hightemperature $(400-1200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ desulfurization potential of oxides of 28 elements with computational free energy minimizations of a C-H-N-O-S elemental system in the presence of the metal oxide using temperature-dependent free energies from the literature.111 Eleven candidate solids, based upon the metals Fe, $Z\mathrm{n},$ Mo, Mn, V, Ca, Sr, Ba, Co, $\mathrm{Cu,}$ , and W, showed thermodynamic feasibility (at least $95\%$ desulfurization and solid stability) for high-temperature desulfurization. Another simulation study at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with feed containing $12.5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ $42.5\%$ CO, $32.175\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ $12.5\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ , and $3250\mathrm{\ppm}\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ showed that oxides of Zn, Mo, Mn, W, Sn, Co, Cu, Ni, and Fe should be successful in reducing the residual $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ content to 0.03, 0.6, 2.5, 3.4, 19, 24, 39, 41, and $91\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , respectively.112
Rodriguez et al. studied adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ on a series of metal oxide surfaces using synchrotron-based high-resolution photoemission.113,114 It was observed that the smaller the band gap in the oxide, the bigger its reactivity toward S-containing molecules (see Figure 3). $A b$ initio self-consistent field (SCF) theory calculations and experimental data indicated that the reactivity of an oxide mainly depends on how well its bands mix with the orbitals of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ or HS.113 Oxides possessing a large ionicity, like $\mathrm{{Al}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{{\bf{Mg}O}_{\mathrm{{\bar{\tau}}}}}$ , displayed the lowest reactivity toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{.}}$ , indicating that the electrostatic interactions between the dipole of $\mathrm{\bar{H}}_{2}S$ and the ionic field generated by the charges in an oxide may play only a secondary role in the adsorption process.114 As can be seen from Figure 3, $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ interacts strongly with $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ . Even before this study, the most significant evolution of the Claus process, the Superclaus process, employed an $\alpha$ -alumina support coated with a mixture of iron and chromium oxides as the catalyst.115 However, due to the severe toxicity of chromia, continuing research to develop new catalysts for this important reaction is underway.116 CuO and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ have band gaps of 1.2 and $2.1\ \mathrm{{\eV},}$ respectively; the contribution by Rodriguez et al. should imply better desulfurization using $\mathtt{C u O}$ compared to $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Roomtemperature experimental screening of oxides of Ag, Cu, $Z\mathrm{n},$ Co, Ni, Ca, Mn, and Sn showed a conversion of $66\%$ for $\mathtt{C u O}$ but <10% for the other oxides.117
Several DFT studies on $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ reaction with oxides of $\mathrm{Zn,^{118,119}~{\cal~F}e,^{120}~{\cal~C}e,^{121}~{\cal~T}i,^{122}~{\cal~M}n,^{123}~{\cal~V},^{124,125}~{\cal~C}u,^{126,127}}$ $\mathrm{Co},^{128}$ and $\mathbf{Mg}^{129}$ have been carried out in the past decade. While these studies provide some details of possible reaction mechanisms for each metal oxide, a comparative study of different pure and mixed oxides for adsorption and regeneration from first-principles calculations, at the same level of theory, can provide insights into optimal metal oxide selection. Jia et al. carried out a comparative study of the reactions of ${\mathbf{M}}_{m}{\mathbf{O}}_{n}^{-}$ ( $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{Fe},$ Co, Ni, $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Cu},}\end{array}$ and $Z\mathbf{n}$ ) with $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ using mass spectrometry and DFT calculations.130 The authors tested several density functionals by calculating the bond enthalpies of $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O},$ , M−S, HS−H, and $S{\mathrm{-H}}$ species, and except for $Z\mathrm{n}{-}S_{;}$ , the B3LYP functional was found to be generally in better agreement with the experimental enthalpies. Adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ by $\mathrm{{znO}}$ is stoichiometric at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ but falls off rapidly as the temperature is reduced.131 Since this is an exothermic reaction, this decline in conversion is due to slower reaction kinetics. Baird et al. reported $91\%$ conversion of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ at room temperature,132 while Xue et al. reported only a $1\%$ conversion.117 These studies suggest the possible implications of synthesis recipes on the size, shape, and surface area of the metal oxide particles that can, in turn affect the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ breakthrough capacity. In fact, there are several reports exploring desulfurization using different $\mathrm{znO}$ morphologies.133−138 While it is hard to draw a head-to-head comparison
between independent studies due to different gas velocities, reactor volume, feed compositions, and definitions of breakthrough concentration, data for these are compiled in Table 5. The $\%$ utilization for a metal oxide-based adsorbent is defined as the ratio of the actual amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorbed to the theoretical amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorbed by complete conversion of metal oxide to metal sulfide, multiplied by 100. Note that the value can change depending on the choice of the oxidation state of the metal in its oxide and sulfide forms. This number can be greater than 100 due to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorbing without chemical reaction with the metal oxide (see, for instance, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption on metal oxides impregnated on high-surface-area silica supports).
The reaction between $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and a metal oxide in the absence of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ belongs to the class of non-catalytic gas−solid reactions. These reactions have been most commonly analyzed using either the unreacted shrinking core model $(\mathrm{\dot{U}S C M})^{139}$ or the grain model.140 USCM is built on the assumption that the reaction is much faster than the diffusion and, hence, the reaction occurs at the solid reactant surface. This model clearly assumes inward propagation of the reaction front. In the grain model, each of the large number of spherical grains in a solid pellet is assumed to react independently according to the USCM. Early reports on sulfidation kinetics of $\mathrm{{znO}}$ , employing the grain model with constant or varying properties (size changes due to sintering and reaction) of the adsorbent, agreed quantitatively with experiments.141,142
From the time when empirical commercialization of $\mathrm{znO}$ as a desulfurization sorbent took precedence until the early 1990s, very little was known about the structural changes and the mechanism for this reaction. Using in situ sulfur K-edge X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) in the soft X-ray region $_{<5000}$ $\mathrm{eV},$ , Evans and co-workers first showed that the reaction of $\mathrm{{znO}}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ occurs via island formation of $Z\mathrm{n}S$ on the oxide surface and, as the reaction proceeds, eventually bulk regions of $Z\mathrm{n}S$ are formed.143,144 Since there was no apparent shift in the edge energies of the $s\kappa$ -edge spectra, it was concluded that the oxidation state of the sulfur remains as $S^{2-}$ throughout the process.143 The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and X-ray diffraction (XRD) data for the final states of the adsorbent confirmed the theory of a two-phase material consisting of crystalline $\mathrm{znO}$ (wurtzite) and a less ordered phase of $Z\mathrm{n}S$ (wurtzite), in spite of the fact that $z_{\mathrm{nS}}$ blend structure is thermodynamically more stable.144 The reader is also referred to a review paper by Samokhvalov et al. on the low-temperature desulfurization using $\mathrm{{znO}}$ .145
Until recently, $\mathrm{{znO}}$ sulfidation models assumed inward outward grow2th mechanism (see Figure 4).146,147 Using diffusion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{,}}$ but Neveux et al. showed evidence for an transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and electron tomography, the authors observed voids inside the $Z\mathrm{n}S$ particle, suggesting that the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ species diffuse through the product $Z\mathrm{n}S$ layer. Since the external particle surface increases during the course of the reaction, the decrease in reaction rate with time cannot be explained if the ratedetermining step (RDS) is located at the external surface (adsorption, desorption, external interface reaction). Thus, the authors proposed that diffusion through the $Z\mathrm{n}S$ layer is most likely to be the RDS. Eight elementary steps of the sulfidation reaction were proposed, with the outward growth of $Z\mathrm{n}S$ and diffusion of zinc and oxygen through ZnS.147 The authors derived a rate expression that also considered the evolution of the voids by a nucleation−growth mechanism, and the model predictions agreed quantitatively with experiments.
To enhance the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capacity at lower temperatures, another approach of varying the $\mathrm{znO}$ morphology involves doping with other metallic species (see Table 6). $117,\bar{1}\bar{3}1,132,148-153$ Skrzypski et al. studied mixed $\mathrm{{Cu-Zn}}$ oxides and found a 6-fold increase in capacity at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ by doping $6\mathrm{mol\%}$ Cu.148 XPS of pristine $\mathrm{Cu}_{0.03}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.97}\mathrm{O}$ showed a Cu $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ peak $(933.5\ \mathrm{eV})$ , characteristic of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ surrounded by oxygen; after only partial sulfidation, this peak shifted to lower energy $(932.6~\mathrm{~eV})$ , indicating the appearance of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ in a sulfide environment. The sulfidation mechanism of these mixed oxides was further investigated using TEM and in situ synchrotron XRD.150 While non-doped $\mathrm{{znO}}$ at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ resulted in void formation at the $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnS}$ interface, Cu-doped $\mathrm{{ZnO}}$ showed no cavity formation; this suggests an inward growth mechanism where $S^{2-}$ anions diffuse from the external surface to the internal $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnS}$ interface and exchange with $\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ anions. This radical change in mechanism was attributed to the presence of sulfur vacancies in the $Z\mathrm{n}S$ layer due to partial replacement of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ by ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ ; these anionic vacancies accelerate the diffusion of sulfur to yield higher conversions of $\mathrm{{Cu-Zn}}$ oxides. XRD measurements showed that the size of the $Z\mathrm{n}S$ crystallites formed from nondoped $\mathrm{{znO}}$ remains constant during the reaction. In contrast, the pronounced growth of $\mathtt{C u}$ -doped samples enhances the mobility of sulfur anions in Cu-containing $Z\mathrm{n}S$ particles.
Jiang et al. also studied $\mathrm{{Cu-Zn}}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu{-}Z n{-}A l}}$ mixed metal oxides prepared via co-precipitation $(\mathsf{c p})$ or multi-precipitation $\left(\mathrm{{mp}}\right)$ methods.149 Better dispersion by aluminum results in smaller mean crystalline sizes and higher specific surface area of Al-containing sorbents; breakthrough sulfur capacities also improve as a result of reducing the thickness of the potential sulfide shell on the outer layer of the oxide, in turn reducing the diffusion path length. The Cu-rich adsorbents were more suitable for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption than the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -rich adsorbents, with mp $\mathrm{-Cu_{80}Z n_{10}A l_{10}}$ reaching nearly complete conversion at 80 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . XRD of sulfidated samples revealed that the CuS peaks were much more dominant compared to the $\mathtt{C u O}$ peaks, but high-intensity $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ peaks persisted for sulfidated $Z\mathrm{n}$ -rich samples. This suggests that $\mathrm{Cu}S$ penetrated deep into the crystal, while $Z\mathrm{n}S$ likely formed only surface layers. Since the anionic sites in $\mathrm{{znO}}$ need to rearrange from hexagonal to cubic lattice on sulfidation, while those in $\mathtt{C u O}$ need only to transform from monoclinic to hexagonal, a faster sulfidation rate was proposed for $\mathtt{C u O}$ than that for $\mathrm{{znO}}$ . Similar structural arguments were invoked for $Z\mathrm{n-Co}$ oxides (discussed below) by Baird et al.132 It should be noted here that the sulfidation reaction in the study by Jiang et al. was carried out at $40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},^{149}$ as opposed to a much higher temperature $(370~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ in the study by Hayter et al. (discussed above)144 that reported the wurtzite (hexagonal) phase of $Z\mathrm{n}S$ . This suggests that temperature can play a role in stabilizing different crystal structures of the product metal sulfide, which in turn can influence the overall sulfidation kinetics.
Co-precipitated mixed oxides showed smaller particle sizes, higher specific surface areas, and a higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capacity compared to their impregnated counterparts that are synthesized by immersing the parent oxide in a salt solution of the dopant metal and calcining this precursor at ∼350 °C.131 These authors also studied adsorption on single-phase $\mathrm{Co-}$ $\mathrm{Zn-Al-O}$ prepared from co-precipitated hydroxycarbonate precursors (hydrotalcite-type structure).151 The presence of aluminum increased the specific surface areas, but the modified compounds did not adsorb significantly more $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ per metallic $(\mathrm{Zn},\ \mathrm{Co})$ species. $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption in $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-Zn}$ oxides with varying $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn}$ atomic ratios showed that the increase in $\%$ utilization correlates well with the increase in surface area; the main function of Co doping seems to be increasing the surface area by reducing the particle size.132 Thermodynamically, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ has a more negative free energy for sulfidation than $\mathrm{znO}$ . Additionally, sulfiding cubic $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ normal spinel to spinel $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ or $\mathrm{Co}_{8}\mathrm{S}_{9}$ will require lesser lattice rearrangement than that for hexagonal $\mathrm{{znO}}$ to cubic $Z\mathrm{n}S$ .
Westmoreland et al. showed that the intrinsic reaction rate of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ with $\mathbf{MnO}$ over a temperature range of $300{-}800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is approximately 1 order of magnitude greater than that with ${\mathrm{ZnO}}_{\cdot}$ , even though its surface area was an order of magnitude smaller.154 Higher conversions were observed for mixed oxides of $Z\mathrm{n}$ containing Fe, Ni, Co, Mn, Cu, Al, ${\mathrm{Ti}},$ and $\mathrm{Zr}$ at room temperature, especially for $\mathrm{Zn}_{2.9}\mathrm{MnO}_{4.2},$ but the reason for poor conversion for pure Mn3O4 solid is not yet clear.117 Regeneration of $Z\mathrm{n}$ −Ti-based mixed metal oxides using $20\%$ $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{He}$ at $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ showed broad XRD peaks of very low intensity, indicating transformation to an amorphous material.152 Doping this oxide with Mn, $\mathrm{Cu,}$ or Mo led to an improvement in $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ uptake for the regenerated solid: $41\mathrm{mg/g}$ for $10\mathrm{Mn}\mathrm{-}45\mathrm{Zn}\mathrm{-}45\mathrm{Ti}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{O}$ using a feed of $600~\mathrm{ppm}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{,}}$ , $25\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ and $7.5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in helium. The presence of $1\%\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the feed leads to a significantly higher capacity $\left(160\ \mathrm{\mg/g}\right)$ . Davidson et al. have demonstrated the autocatalytic role of water during desulfurization on $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , suggesting a mechanism involving proton transfer from $\mathrm{H},S$ to chemisorbed OH groups from hydration of the surface.137,155 Addition of Fe showed a pronounced effect of $\mathrm{Fe/Mn}$ molar ratio on the morphology of the $\mathrm{Fe-Mn{-}Z n{\cdot}}$ −Ti mixed oxide crystallites.116 $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ uptakes of $82~\mathrm{mg/g}$ and $17~\mathrm{mg/g}$ were observed for $\mathrm{5Fe-15Mn{-}}40\mathrm{Zn{-}}$ 40Ti−O and $20\mathrm{Fe}{-}40\mathrm{Zn}{-}40\mathrm{Ti}{-}\mathrm{O},$ respectively; in this case, $1\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ led to only a small increase in capacity to $100~\mathrm{{mg/g}.}$ Mössbauer spectroscopy is sensitive to changes in the energy levels of an atomic nucleus in response to its environment; quadrupole splitting $(\Delta)$ is induced by an electric field gradient of nearby electrons. Changes in $\Delta$ during sulfidation and regeneration in the case of $5\mathrm{Fe}-15\mathrm{Mn}-40\mathrm{Zn}-40\mathrm{Ti}-\mathrm{O}$ solid, compared to no changes for $20\mathrm{Fe}{-}40\mathrm{Zn}{-}40\mathrm{Ti}{-}\mathrm{O}$ solid, suggest that Mn makes the mixed oxide structures more susceptible to structural distortions, in turn enhancing $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption. Regeneration in $20\%$ $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in He at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ resulted in over $80\%$ of capacity recovered for the Fecontaining solids, while regeneration at $750~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ halved the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capacity. The structure was preserved for the regenerated $5\mathrm{Fe}-15\mathrm{Mn}-40\mathrm{Zn}-40\mathrm{Ti}-\mathrm{O}$ solid with clear peaks in the XRD pattern.
Liu et al. investigated copper-based cp-mixed oxides (see Table 7) and observed high capacities for Fe-doped oxides.157
Capacities were $10{-}40\%$ higher for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in bulk $\Nu_{2}$ compared to the case of bulk $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ suggesting some degree of competitive adsorption with $\mathrm{CO}_{2};$ the inhibition effect was lower for doped $\mathtt{C u O}$ compared to pure $\mathtt{C u O}$ . The authors report that regenerated $\mathtt{C u O}$ exhibited a breakthrough capacity up to $75\%$ of its initial value when regenerated between 100 and 200 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ but decreased rapidly with further increase of regeneration temperature. The authors explain that $\mathbf{CuS}$ tends to decompose into the more stable $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}S$ when the temperature is above 220 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The authors carried out five adsorption−regeneration cycles for pure $\mathtt{C u O}_{.}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}{-}{\mathrm{Al}}{-}\mathrm{O}_{\mathit{i}} $ , and $\mathrm{Fe{-}C u{-}A{\bar{l}}{-}O}$ with regeneration using air at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . For CuO, the regenerated capacities were maintained at $75\%$ of the fresh adsorbent capacity; however, regenerated capacities decreased for the $\mathrm{Cu-Al-O}$ and $\mathrm{Fe-Cu-Al-O}$ adsorbents and became steady during the last two cycles at $50\%$ and $43\%$ of the fresh capacity, respectively. These studies bring to light the difficulty in regenerating the high capacities of mixed metal oxides; future work in this area should focus not only on high-capacity sorbents but also on better regenerability.
Researchers have investigated $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption in threedimensionally ordered macroporous (3DOM) structures of iron oxide (3DOM-F) and of silica-supported iron oxide (3DOM-FS) prepared by a colloidal crystal templating method (see Table 8).158,159 Highly uniform polystyrene (PS) beads ordered into close-packed domains were used as a template for the growth of the inorganic 3DOM structure; the resulting pore diameters were considerably smaller than the original PS sphere.158 The shrinkage in size resulting from sintering during calcination ranged from 45 to $59\%$ for the 3DOM-F sorbents and from 37 to $45\%$ for the 3DOM-FS sorbents. 3DOM-FS showed somewhat smaller nanoparticles $(\boldsymbol{5}\ \mathrm{nm})$ than 3DOM-F $\left(25{\mathrm{~nm}}\right)$ , which the authors explain as silicon keeping the iron grains separate and preventing the latter from growing larger during calcination. Depending on the macropore diameter, these structures have variable surface areas, ranging from 16 to
$44~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for 3DOM-F and from 80 to $220~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for 3DOM-FS. Mesopore-sized void spaces between the crystallites are observed in the TEM images and also from the pore size distribution (20−33 and $6{-}17~\mathrm{{nm}}$ for 3DOM-F and 3DOMFS, respectively) obtained from the ${\bf N}_{2}$ isotherms. It can be seen that the $\%$ utilization of Fe improves with increase in macropore diameter up to about $150~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ due to possible reduction in diffusion limitations, but further increasing the macropore diameter leads to a lowering of Fe utilization. Nearly complete regeneration of 3DOM-FS samples using $2\%$ $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was shown for three cycles. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) illustrated that, despite some cracking, the macroporosity and ordering of the structure was still intact and the overall 3DOM structure was retained after second regeneration.
Huang et al. studied the effect of Fe:Si ratio from 1 to 16 (and also the case of no Si), temperature, and humidity on the low-temperature desulfurization performance of 3DOM structures.159 Breakthrough capacities showed an increase with increase in Fe:Si ratio up to 8; low capacity at even higher Fe:Si ratios was attributed to the presence of $\alpha{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ enhanced the dispersion of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},$ which stabilized the $\gamma-$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and hindered its transformation to $\alpha{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ during calcination. The authors further explain that the vacancy site in the lattice of $\gamma{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},$ accompanied by its lower density, larger surface area, and smaller grain size, facilitates ion diffusion. Relative humidity of ${\sim}10\%$ favored $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal. It was also found that conventional regeneration at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using $5\%$ $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ resulted in a drastic loss of capacity for the regenerated material. As an alternative regeneration technique, the authors proposed an in situ regeneration process by adding $5\%$ $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to the feed stream (desulfurization and regeneration occur at the same time); this resulted in breakthrough capacities twice those observed when $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was absent from the inlet gas. In the case of conventional regeneration, a significant fraction of $\gamma{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ transformed to $\alpha{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3},$ while no such change was observed for the case where $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was added to the feed. It is unclear if this simultaneous desulfurization−regeneration process is resulting in a transformation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ to $S\mathrm{O}_{2};$ monitoring only $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentration in the outlet stream may hint at high $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ breakthrough capacity, but monitoring $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration may reveal that little or no sulfur is adsorbed in such a process.
In another study, Wang et al. synthesized 3DOM structures of $\mathrm{{znO}}$ and silica, with different weight percent of $\mathrm{{znO}}$ ranging from 23 to $70\%$ .160 Samples exhibited a typical amorphous structure, except 3DOM-SZ73, which showed peaks corresponding to $\mathrm{{znO}}$ in the XRD pattern. Highest utilization of $69\%$ was observed for the 3DOM structure with $50~\mathrm{wt\%~ZnO}_{\cdot}$ ; nearly $70\%$ of this capacity was retained for four regeneration cycles (air at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ ). These adsorbents hold significant promise for desulfurization at ambient temperature, and investigation of structural stability over several regeneration cycles will help reveal the true potential of these sorbents for commercial application.
Even though cobalt oxide is one of the thermodynamically more favorable metals for desulfurization of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{,}}$ , attempts at making it a major component for removing $\mathrm{\mathrm{H}}_{2}S$ are limited.132,162 Due to larger particle sizes, bulk cobalt oxide showed slower sulfidation and incomplete conversion even at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ but when it was supported on either $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ or ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2},$ higher rates and complete conversion could be observed even at ${300}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ primarily due to smaller grain size.162 $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}.$ - supported $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ showed lower activation barriers, and it may serve as an effective $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal sorbent, even at lower temperatures. Pahalagedara et al. investigated mesoporous cobalt oxides $(\mathrm{m-Co-}X,$ where $X=150_{\cdot}$ , 250, 350, or $450\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the calcination temperature) as desulfurization sorbents and could achieve $100\%$ capacity at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .161 The reason for breakthrough capacities higher than $100\%$ metal utilization for mesoporous $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ and Mn is not clear but may be attributed to physically adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ beyond the chemically reacted capacity. The authors also investigated other mesoporous materials $(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},$ $\mathtt{C u O}$ , $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3},$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},$ ) that showed superior activities compared to their nonporous analogues (as high as $200\ \mathrm{\times})$ ). Using field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM), the mesostructure was shown to be preserved after sulfidation, but the mesostructure collapsed during calcination at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and regeneration at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air resulted in a $45\%$ drop in capacity. An alternative regeneration technique for $\mathrm{znO}$ catalysts using a 3:1 $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ mixture at 800 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ resulted in nearly $100\%$ capacity regeneration without loss of porosity, as opposed to only about $10\%$ regeneration and significant increase in bulk density of the adsorbent when air was used at the same temperature.163 A significant scope still remains for developing better regeneration procedures for metal oxides that retain not only the adsorption capacity reversibility but also the structural reversibility. The latter is more important from the perspective of adsorbent bed stability to prevent attrition of the solids that will increase the pressure drop across the bed.
## 3.2. Metals
Early literature focused on $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ poisoning of metal catalysts and the threshold concentration below which $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ will not unduly poison a given metal catalyst. McCarty and Wise investigated the thermodynamics of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ chemisorption on different metallic catalysts over a wide range of temperatures and reported the order of increasing activity toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $50\%$ surface coverage to be $\mathrm{Ni}>\mathrm{Co}>\mathrm{Ru}>\mathrm{Fe}>\mathrm{Pt.}^{164-167}$ Using magnetization measurements, Den Besten and Selwood showed that $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is dissociatively chemisorbed on the Ni surface.168 The enthalpy of adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ on Ni was measured to be about $130{-}\mathrm{i}60~\mathrm{\kJ/mo{}\bar{l}}$ 164,169 Due to such high enthalpies of adsorption, adsorbent regeneration will result in a very expensive natural gas desulfurization process. Metal adsorbents may be useful as a final polishing step to limit the energy duty for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption/regeneration. For example, they can be useful in certain applications such as direct-methane fuel cells170 to achieve ultra-low sulfur content methane from pretreated natural gas.
Usually, when metals are employed for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal, they are used as sacrificial sorbents since, during a regeneration process, metals will have a strong tendency to sinter and aggregate, resulting in a complete loss of surface area and active sites. By isolating and stabilizing small metal particles in cubic structured mesoporous silica, Li et al. were probably the first to demonstrate the regeneration of the sulfur-loaded adsorbents with minimal sintering (see Figure 5).171 Adsorption was carried out at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using a coal syngas composition of $23\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ $29\%$ CO, $8\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ $30\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , $10\%$ He, and $10\ \mathrm{ppm}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ and regeneration was carried out at $550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using a five-cycle alternating oxidation−reduction treatment of pure $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $10\%$ air in Ar with a short Ar purge in between. A fully regenerable capacity of $7~\mathrm{mg}$ of $s/\mathrm{g}$ adsorbent over five adsorption− regeneration cycles was achieved for $16.6\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ Ni loaded on SBA-16, with the ratio of chemisorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ to $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}$ being 0.07. It is unclear, however, whether $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption was carried out on the hydrogen-regenerated form of the adsorbent or on the airregenerated form that would result in a metal oxide.
## 3.3. Metal−Organic Frameworks
MOFs are porous structures resulting from coordination of metal ions or clusters to organic ligands.172 Bare metals exist at most as nanoparticles formed out of clusters of several thousands of atoms and suffer from activity loss by way of sintering during high-temperature regeneration. MOFs provide a scheme for isolating the metal atoms or the metal oxide clusters by being chemically coordinated within a porous framework. This allows design of adsorbents where not only each metal atom exclusively interacts with a single adsorbate molecule but also each of these interaction sites may be regenerated without significant loss of activity. Additionally, appropriately functionalized organic ligands can also contribute in promoting the selectivity toward the desired gas molecule. MOFs offer a large parameter space to rationally design the size, shape, and functionality of pores with high adsorption and/or diffusion selectivity toward the molecule of interest. In addition to selectivity, performance and structural reversibility on regeneration are important factors in judging the performance. The presence of polar impurities such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ on the adsorption performance as well as stability of the adsorbent structure can also play determining roles in the successful application of MOFs for desulfurization applications. Typical pore diameters in MOFs are in the microporous regime ranging from 3 to $20\mathrm{~\AA~}^{173}$ although more recently, MOFs with pore sizes as high as 85 Å have been synthesized for inclusion of large biological molecules.174 Due to the highly porous nature of most MOFs, their lower conductivity $(0.3\mathrm{W/m}\mathrm{\cdotK}$ for MOF5 at $300~\mathrm{K}^{175}$ ) compared to that of crystalline materials in general can be a potential concern for strong host−guest interactions. Desulfurization using MOFs has been reviewed in the past;14,15,176,177 here we try to summarize some of these topics, provide our interpretation, and identify areas for further investigation.
Hamon et al. carried out the first experimental study of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption in MOFs; these included the small-pore (SP) MIL$53(\mathrm{Al}^{\mathrm{III}},\ \mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}},\ \mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}})$ and $\mathrm{{MIL-47(V^{IV})}}$ $(\sim11\ \mathrm{\AA})$ and the largepore (LP) MIL- $100\mathrm{(Cr)}$ and MIL-101 $\langle\mathrm{Cr}\rangle$ (25 and $30\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ cage diameter and $4.8\times5.8$ and $12.5\times12.5$ Å pore apertures, respectively).178 SP MILs are built from corner-sharing chains of metal oxide $(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{IV}}\mathbf{O}_{6})$ or hydroxide $\left(\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathbf{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\right)$ octahedra connected through terephthalate moieties $(\dot{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}\approx1000~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ (see Figure 6). LP MILs comprise of trimers of chromium octahedra linked with trimesate (MIL-100) or terephthalate (MIL-101) $(S_{\mathrm{BET}}>2000~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ . MIL-100, MIL-101, and MIL47 showed the type-I-shaped (no adsorption step) isotherm, indicating that these are rigid structures; $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}53(\mathrm{Al},\mathrm{Cr},\mathrm{Fe})$ exhibited two-step adsorption isotherms, which the authors proposed to be caused by the polar $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecules interacting strongly with the OH groups of the inorganic chains at the pore opening, leading to closure of the pores at low loading. This hypothesis was later claimed to be verified using infrared (IR) spectroscopy and molecular simulations.179 It should be noted here that, for the breathing MOF $\left(\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Cr})\right)$ , isotherms were simulated for two different rigid MOF structures, representing the narrow and the large-pore regimes in the experimental adsorption isotherm. By imposing the experimental fractions of each pore type as a function of pressure, a composite isotherm was calculated for the simulated isotherms. While this strategy yields a good match with the experimental data, the study did not investigate the ability to predict the structural transformation. Measurement of excess $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption before and after high-pressure $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption followed by vacuum desorption showed full recovery of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity for MIL-47(V) and MIL-53(Al,Cr), partial recovery for MIL-100 and MIL-101, and complete loss of capacity for MIL53(Fe) due to its decomposition into iron sulfide. At $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 20 bar, MIL-101 showed an unprecedented $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ loading of 38.4 $\mathrm{mmol/g};$ MIL-53(Al,Cr), MIL-47, and MIL-100 showed saturation loadings of 11.8, 13.1, 14.6, and $16.7\ \mathrm{\mmol/g},$ respectively, which are also quite high compared to those of other $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorbents. However, in cases such as off-shore natural gas sweetening, where having a smaller footprint is important, volumetrically bulky porous MOFs suffer a drawback. Adsorption being an exothermic process, sharp increases in temperatures for MOFs with ultra-high capacity raise concerns about bed stability in the absence of adequate heat transfer. Attempts to compute thermal conductivities of MOFs in the presence of adsorbed gases are starting to elucidate that the thermal conductivity of MOFs decreases with increasing pore sizes.180,181
Vaesen et al. explored functionalization of MOFs for desulfurization application by comparing amino-functionalized titanium terephthalate MIL-125(Ti)- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ versus its parent MIL-125(Ti) analogue.182 The frameworks consists of tetrahedral (4.7 Å) and octahedral (10.7 Å) cages, accessible through triangular windows of $5\mathrm{-}7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . The cage diameters for the functionalized material are slightly smaller those of the parent material (6 and $12\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ for the tetrahedral and octahedral cages, respectively). Grand Canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations were carried out using force fields that were shown to yield a decent agreement with the single-component experimental adsorption isotherms and enthalpies. The GCMC-predicted binary $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity values (at 10 bar, $30\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and $y_{\mathrm{H}_{2}S}~=~0.001,$ ) for MIL-125(Ti) and MIL$125\mathrm{(Ti)}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were reported to be 40 and 67, respectively. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption enthalpy for these materials $({\sim}30\mathrm{~kJ/mol})$ is comparable to that for the aluminosilicate zeolite 4A $(\sim35\mathrm{kJ}/\$ mol).183 These MOF materials are thermally stable up to 300 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , but only up to $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in water vapor. Their performance and structural stability in the presence of both $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ require further investigation. Furthermore, the moderately high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity $(\sim7)$ allows for envisaging a one-step process for the concomitant elimination of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ . Obtaining separate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -rich and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ -rich streams unnecessarily increases the separation costs if the end goal for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is high-pressure reinjection into empty wells, instead of the conventional Claus process for sulfur recovery.
Yang et al. studied $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption in MIL-68(Al) at room temperature using molecular simulations and experiments.184 This framework consists of two kinds of channels, triangular $(6.0{-}6.4\mathring\mathrm{A})$ and hexagonal $(16-17\mathrm{\AA})$ , running along the c axis (see Figure 7). It was found that only some of the hydroxyls were available for interaction, as evidenced by the computed isotherm, obtained by blocking the triangular channels, matching exactly with the experimental isotherm. It can also be observed that the saturation loadings are less sensitive to the choice of force field parameters than the Henry’s law region slope. This partially activated material was fully regenerable for five consecutive cycles with a pure-component $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capacity of $12\mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 12 bar and an adsorption enthalpy of ${\sim}21\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ . Even if the material can be fully activated, the $25\%$ additional saturation capacity, that is predicted from simulations, may or may not be accessible; the difference in the adsorbed amounts of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ for the material activated at 260 or $330~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was negligible. Although MIL68(Al) is a polymorph of MIL-53(Al) (described above), unlike the latter, the former does not seem to exhibit framework flexibility, as evidenced by its type-I adsorption isotherm. This brings to light the importance of MOF framework topology in addition to the choice of the metallic cluster and the organic ligands, further iterating the importance of powerful screening of adsorbents using existing and new computational techniques that capture these subtle differences.
Cu-BTC (or HKUST-1 or MOF-199) comprises of an intersecting three-dimensional system of large square-shaped pores $(9\check{\times}9\hat{\mathrm{~A~}})$ and small tetrahedral pockets $(\hat{5}\ \hat{\mathrm{A}})$ formed with copper as the inorganic metal and benzene-1,3,5- tricarboxylic acid (BTC), also known as trimesic acid, as the organic linker (see Figure 8) with a reasonable degree of thermal stability (up to $240~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ '$ ).185 In the past few years, several research groups have investigated various aspects of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption in this material.186−192 Petit et al. first studied HKUST-1 and its composites with graphite oxide under ambient conditions using 1000 ppm $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in moist air.186 The authors commented that bed pre-humidification does not prevent the adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ $\left(2.7{\mathrm{~mmol/g}}\right)$ ; on the contrary, it favors its retention via dissolution in the water film. Additional $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ measurements in absence of moisture will help better understand the role of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The XRD pattern revealed that exposure to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ results in a collapse of the MOF structure. From the decrease in $\mathrm{\pH}$ on $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption and from Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy, it was concluded that the BTC ligands were released (no more coordinated to copper) to their acidic form.
Watanabe and Sholl investigated chemisorption of several molecules, including $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ in $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC using plane wave periodic DFT.187 $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ showed similar binding energies of 51 and $47\ \mathrm{kJ/mol},$ respectively. Single-component GCMC adsorption simulations at room temperature, computed by restricting the chemisorbed molecules in the geometries obtained from prior DFT calculations, showed that the onset pressure for $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption is nearly 3 orders of magnitude lower than that for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ . The influence of physisorbed molecules on the adsorption isotherm was not considered in this study. $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ models in the literature, trained to reproduce the bulk liquid densities and vapor pressures, significantly overestimate the gas-phase dipole moment of $\mathrm{\iH}_{2}S$ . GutiérrezSevillano et al. developed new $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ models to test whether a model with an accurate gas-phase dipole moment could predict a strong interaction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ with Cu-BTC, in line with the experimental observation.188 The simulations used generic force fields for the MOF structure that have been shown to perform reasonably well for some of the other adsorption studies in MOFs.184 Additionally, their hybrid DFT calculations showed that indeed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ has a higher affinity for the copper sites (extra $3.3\mathrm{\kJ/mol},$ ) compared to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ . Ab initio molecular dynamics (MD) studies of the molecules adsorbed on the model cluster showed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to be closer to the copper center compared to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ by about $2.6\mathrm{\AA}.$ Another hybrid DFT study, with a damped $1/\dot{r}^{6}$ term to account for London dispersion, showed that the interaction energies for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption on $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC are $-60.9$ and $-52.2~\mathrm{{\kJ/mol}},$ respectively, reiterating the dominance of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .189 Different theoretical treatments are in agreement that the interaction of $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC with $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is weaker than that with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , thus failing to explain the experimental observation that $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ converts $\mathtt{C u}$ in Cu-BTC to black CuS in the presence of moisture.186 An important point to note here is that the theoretical studies only considered the host−guest interaction; however, $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ reacts chemically with copper to form $\mathbf{CuS}_{\cdot}$ , and the thermodynamics of product formation have not been attempted in any of the theoretical studies reported so far.
Li et al. experimentally investigated the same system by activating the material before carrying out H2S adsorption.190 They found that the breakthrough capacity increased by $38\%$ on activation at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ compared to the unactivated sample and decreased by $10\%$ if the sample was activated further at 200 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The breakthrough capacity also increased by $40\%$ when the adsorption temperature was raised from 30 to $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Both of these observations suggest that there is a competitive adsorption between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ and lowering the adsorbed quantity of moisture, either with an increased activation temperature or with an increased adsorption temperature, increases the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capacity. Contrary to this inference, Peterson et al. observed no change in $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption capacity under dry ( $0\%$ relative humidity) and wet ( $80\%$ relative humidity) conditions.191 In spite of a lot of research effort to understand the role of moisture in desulfurization using Cu-BTC, this problem remains unresolved.
Eddaoudi et al. synthesized a series of 16 isoretucular (having the same network topology) MOFs (IRMOF- $n$ , $n=1-16)$ based on a skeleton of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF-5, wherein the pore functionality and size were varied without changing the original cubic topology.173 Theoretical investigation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ was performed in IRMOF-1 (or MOF-5) having 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate organic linkers; the heat of adsorption was $\sim-15$ $\mathrm{kJ/mol}.$ , much lower than that for Cu-BTC $\left(\sim-30\mathrm{\kJ/mol}\right)$ due to wider $(11.2\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ pores of IRMOF-1 compared to confined $(\mathsf{\Omega}5\mathrm{\AA})$ tetrahedral-shaped pockets in $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC. IRMOF-1 also results in ${\sim}6\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ weaker binding than $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ , suggesting the difficulty in using this material for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption from moist natural gas.188 Wang et al. studied IRMOF-3 (free pore diameter of 9.6 Å) having 2-amino-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate ligands and showed that the breakthrough capacity increased by $46\%$ on activation at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ compared to that of the unactivated sample and decreased by $93\%$ if the sample was activated further at ${}200\ {}^{\circ}{\bf C}.{}^{193}$ The breakthrough capacity decreased to $19\%$ when the adsorption temperature was increased from 30 to $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . It would be interesting to study comparative $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption in the two MOFs (IRMOF-1 and IRMOF-3) to understand the role of the $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ group in IRMOF-3 for selective binding with acid gases.
Allan et al. studied desulfurization using Ni-CPO-27 and $Z\mathrm{n}\cdot$ - CPO-27, where ${\mathrm{Ni}}/{\mathrm{Zn}}$ cations are linked by 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid organic linkers, forming a honeycomb lattice with channels of ${\sim}1\dot{1}$ Å pore diameter.1 4 In the hydrated state, each metal is coordinated by one $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule, but upon activation at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is removed, leaving the metal in square pyramidal geometry with an open metal site, resulting in $S_{\mathrm{BET}}=1193$ and $379\ \mathrm{{\dot{m}}^{2}/g},$ respectively. $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ exposure did not degrade the crystalline structure of Ni-CPO27 but amorphized $Z\mathbf{n}$ -CPO-27.194,195 Ni-CPO-27 showed a steep rise in loading up to $6.4\mathrm{mmol/g}$ (corresponding to each metal center interacting with one gas molecule, see Figure 9), indicating a sharp chemisorption regime followed by a slower uptake likely due to physisorption up to $12\ \mathrm{\mmol/g}$ at atmospheric pressure. Adsorbents with such high loadings at low pressures are rare, and it may be useful in effectively removing large amounts of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ from gas streams with a low $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ partial pressure. However, the material cannot be fully regenerated at outgassing temperature of $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and a vacuum of $\stackrel{\cdot}{0.1}\mathrm{{Pa}},\stackrel{194}{}$ but it could be nearly completely regenerated at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .195 The authors hint at some damage to the framework by this high-temperature regeneration; additional microscopy and diffraction data for the regenerated samples should help understand the degree of damage and whether the structural damage increases with the number of regeneration cycles. The heat of adsorption is nearly constant $\left(56{-}58~\mathrm{kJ/mol}\right)$ up to a loading of $0.7\ \mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecule per atom of $\mathrm{Ni},$ assuming all Ni atoms are activated. The authors comment that, beyond this loading, the drop in interaction strength can be justified with typical weak physisorption with no specific interaction between the host/guest species. However, it is unclear how the physisorption regime can start without reaching a loading of 1 $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecule per Ni atom, and this could possibly indicate incomplete activation of the MOF ( $\sim70\%$ activated). Compared to other MOFs investigated so far for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption, Ni-CPO-27 shows the highest uptake and high enthalpy of adsorption (indicative of possibly higher selectivity over methane) with structural stability. However, the experimental heat of adsorption of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ for this material is $\mathrm{<-100~kJ/mol}$ , which may or may not make this material an ideal adsorbent for natural gas in the presence of moisture. Nonetheless, the material appears to be very promising and should be investigated for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .
Using GCMC simulations with generic force fields that have not been thoroughly validated for any particular property, Peng and Cao carried out a screening of some select porous carbons, zeolites, and MOFs for a feed containing $0.2\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{;}}$ , $29.8\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $70\%$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at 50 bar and $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .196 Among the MOFs, indium-based rho-zMOF (a zeolite-like MOF with the topology of rho-zeolite) and socMOF showed high selectivity (175 and 100, respectively) and capacity (2.6 and $1.8\ \mathrm{\mmol/g},$ respectively). The capacity for rho-zMOF at temperatures of $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and higher is reported to be $0.5\ \mathrm{mmol/g},$ suggesting a working capacity of about $2\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ with very low temperatures of regeneration. While this is an important discovery, there is a considerable uncertainty in the force-field parameters for such highly charged systems, and experiments will help to confirm the validity of the predictions as regards such high selectivity and capacity as well as stability of MOF in the presence of impurities such as moisture.
Very recently, Belmabkhout et al. investigated $\mathrm{In}^{3+}.$ -, $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ -, ${\mathrm{Ga}}^{3+}.$ -, and $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ -based socMOFs as adsorbents and thin-film membranes for the production of high-quality hydrocarbons 1 $\mathrm{{'}C H}_{4},\mathrm{{C}_{3}H_{8}}$ and ${n\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{4}}\mathrm{H}_{10},$ and olefins) from $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ -containing gas streams. Only $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Ga}}^{3+}$ - and $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ -based socMOFs could preserve the crystal structure on exposure to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S.$ .197 The authors measured pure-component adsorption isotherms for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{.}}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in ${\mathrm{Ga}}^{3+}$ -socMOF at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and found that, unlike $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ showed a steep adsorption. The authors also carried out breakthrough measurements for a ternary mixture containing $5\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{.}}$ , $5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $90\%$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ for six adsorption− desorption cycles with a combination of temperature and vacuum swing regeneration. Optimal regeneration was achieved at $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a helium flow for 8 h with complete recyclability of the adsorbent. Since the mass of adsorbent used for the experiment is unclear (between 0.1 and $\boldsymbol{0.4\ \mathrm{\g}})$ , the reported $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ breakthrough time of $40\ \mathrm{min}$ translates to a breakthrough capacity between 2 and $8\mathrm{mmol/g}$ . $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ breakthrough occurred at nearly 0 and $5\ \mathrm{min}$ , respectively, suggesting very high $\mathrm{H}_{2}S/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivities. The narrow window aperture (4.5 Å) to reach the cages of the of ${\mathrm{Ga}}^{3+}$ -socMOF may have an influence on the breakthrough capacity and selectivity. $\mathbb{A}^{3+}$ -socMOF was shown to be stable up to temperatures as high as $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and relative humidity as high as $95\%$ , and even soaking in liquid water for 2 weeks did not affect the XRD pattern. However, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ stability of the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Ga}}^{3+}$ -socMOF was not reported. These materials show promise for regenerative sour gas sweetening using MOFs.
Stability of adsorbent in the corrosive environment of acid gases in the presence/absence of moisture is arguably the most important test that the adsorbent must meet in order to qualify as a potential candidate for sweetening applications. The notso-strong coordination bond between the metal and the organic ligand in a MOF structure make it very susceptible to hydrolysis and poor stability. Burtch et al. have recently published a very detailed paper in Chemical Reviews on the hydrothermal stability of MOFs.198 Apart from a few studies on acid gas stability of certain MOFs ,199 this area is not very mature for a comprehensive review. Future efforts in this direction will not only help advance the development of adsorbents for commercial purposes but also enhance the fundamental understanding of MOF stability.
Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), a sub-class of MOFs, composed of transition metal ions that replace the silicon/ aluminum atoms in a zeolite topology and organic linkers in place of the oxygen atoms in the zeolite lattice, are a relatively new type of materials that benefit from the structural variety of MOFs and stability of zeolites.200,201 ZIF-8, with a BET surface area of $1630\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ a high thermal stability (up to $550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , and remarkable chemical resistance to boiling alkaline water and organic solvents,201 is one of the very extensively studied ZIF materials in the literature. Recently, Ethiraj et al. investigated the stability of HKUST-1 and ZIF-8 MOFs in an $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ environment using various techniques, including XRD, and observed a significantly higher stability of ZIF-8 over HKUST1.192 Other microporous materials, such as covalent organic frameworks202 and porous aromatic frameworks,203 that are low-density, high-surface-area materials but do not contain any selective metallic sites, can be explored for bulk $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal.
As noted earlier, feed streams with varying overall pressure as well as concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ are likely to require very different sweetening procedures. While low-concentration (10−1000 ppm) breakthrough measurements provide important data for applications such as Claus tail gas cleanup, data at higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ partial pressures are also important for applications involving ultra-sour gas streams. A major difficulty in drawing a fair comparison between different MOFs in the literature for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption arises due to the unavailability of complete adsorption data over a wide range of partial pressures, from a few ppm at atmospheric pressure to $100\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ at several atmospheres in the presence/absence of moisture, different definitions of breakthrough capacity, etc. Future studies should account for factors such as moisture, regeneration for several cycles, and XRD measurements before and after each (or at least, after every few) regeneration.
## 3.4. Zeolites
For several decades, zeolites have been used as catalysts in the petrochemical industry for cracking, isomerization, and alkylation applications. These materials are also widely used for water purification and softening in the detergent industry. Structurally, zeolites are crystalline porous aluminosilicates consisting of a three-dimensional framework of $\mathrm{SiO}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{AlO}_{4}$ tetrahedra. Their pores are defined by their crystal structure and have precise sizes and shapes, allowing them excellent sieving properties at a molecular level. As of today, the database of the International Zeolite Association−Structure Commission identifies 232 unique zeolite frameworks, and each framework is represented by a three-letter code.204 The International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry classifies nanoporous materials into three classes: microporous $\left(<2\mathrm{nm}\right)$ , mesoporous $\left(2-50\mathrm{~\nm}\right)$ , and macroporous $(>50\ \mathrm{\nm})$ . Zeolites are microporous in nature, and depending on the particular framework structure, the maximum diameter of a sphere that can diffuse through the zeolite framework can range from 1 to 8 Å. The maximum diameter of a sphere that can be included in the zeolite structures can range from 3 to $16\mathrm{\AA}$
3.4.1. All-Silica Zeolites. All-silica zeolites have a very low polarity due to negligible amounts of polar cations and silanol defects, imparting a highly hydrophobic character to this class of zeolites.206 Maghsoudi et al. first investigated purecomponent adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in an all-silica zeolite of the chabazite (CHA) framework.207 This material demonstrated a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capacity of $4\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 2 bar, suggesting that all-silica zeolites maybe promising materials for bulk $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal. Shah et al. carried out Monte Carlo simulations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ both pure-component and mixture, over a wide range of temperatures, pressures, and compositions in seven different all-silica zeolites.208 The authors showed quantitative agreement between predictive molecular simulations without any fitting parameters and the tehxepefroirmce nfitealldsdaftoar $\mathrm{\dot{H_{2}S}},^{209}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{\dot{H_{4}}},^{2}$ 1a0gahnsdoutdhieezteaol.i, et.h2u11s Svahlaitdaet nalg. also assessed the application of ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) to pure-component data in order to predict mixture data and found differences as high as $60\%$ in case of some zeolites. In order to probe whether the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ induces $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ coadsorption and reduces the hydrophobic character of all-silica zeolites, binary $\mathrm{H}_{2}S/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ mixture adsorption in MFI was also studied. The simulations showed preferential adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ over $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with a selectivity of about 18, suggesting that these zeolites can be promising materials for sour gas sweetening. In a subsequent study, Shat et al. carried out a large-scale computational screening of the adsorption of binary $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ mixtures in the all-silica forms of 386 zeolitic frameworks (see Figure 10).205 The authors also investigated the adsorption of four- and five-component mixtures at the conditions representing sour gas fields for 16 promising all-silica zeolites selected from binary adsorption selectivities and capacities. It was found that, depending on the fractions of $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6},$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ different sorbents allow for optimal $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal and hydrocarbon recovery.
Liu et al. carried out a multi-scale modeling study for combined material screening and process optimization to reduce the overall process cost with atomistic and mesoscopic scales for the selection of zeolites and the macroscopic scale for the selection of optimal conditions for pressure swing adsorption.212 Several high-performing zeolites were identified, and zeolite ABW ranks as one of the top materials for the removal of both $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (acid gases) from natural gas. There is a considerable degree of overlap between the zeolites identified from the two screening studies in the literature for high $\mathrm{H}_{2}S/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivities.205,212 The best-performing zeolites will likely always be from this pool of structures identified with high selectivity over methane; however, the optimal adsorbent will depend on the feed conditions (wet/dry, lean/rich in light hydrocarbons, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ proportions).
Of all the top-performing structures presented in Figure 10, CAS, DOH, ITE, ITH, ITW, ${\mathrm{~\bf~MEL,~}}$ MEP, MFI, MTN, RRO, RWR, and SFF frameworks have been synthesized in their allsilica form, IWV with a Si/Al ratio of 29, SEW with a $\mathrm{Si/B}$ ratio of 13, and ABW, BIK, EDI, EPI, GIS, JBW, LTJ, LTL, MER, MON, MOZ, PAU, PHI, and UFI with a low to moderate Si/Al ratio.205 Although DOH, MEP, and MTN show a high selectivity, it is unlikely that the pores in these structures with only six-membered ring openings will allow accessibility to the selective sites. Development of experimental syntheses for the all-silica defect-free forms of these top-performing zeolite topologies can further the development of these hydrophobic adsorbents for the bulk acid gas removal from ultra-sour gas fields.
3.4.2. Aluminosilicate Zeolites. Aluminosilicate zeolites are crystalline microporous frameworks, based on a threedimensional network of silica and alumina, that can accommodate a variety of cations such as $\mathrm{Na^{+},K^{+},C a^{2+},}$ etc. In addition to the precise sizes and shapes of pores in all-silica zeolites that allow them excellent sieving properties at a molecular level, the cations in aluminosilicate zeolites may allow the possibility of strong selective interactions with the desired polar gas molecules. There is a significant amount of literature exploring the Claus reaction between $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce elemental sulfur using cation-exchanged zeolites.213,214 While this is a very important aspect of sour gas sweetening, it has been reviewed in the past,20 and we will focus here on the interaction of these zeolites with $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in the absence of oxygen. Ozekmekci et al. have presented a short review on $\mathrm{\bar{H}}_{2}S$ removal using zeolites.215 We try to expand on this subject here. Identifying the adsorption mechanism of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in cationexchanged zeolites has been a challenge, whether it is a physisorption, chemisorption by coordination to the cations, or a chemical reaction forming $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ using either framework oxygen or gas-phase oxygen impurities or possibly even metal oxides of the extra-framework cations. In an attempt to understand the adsorption mechanism, several zeolitic systems based on faujasite (FAU; NaX, NaY, HY), LTA (NaA, CaNaA, ZnNaA, MnNaA, CoNaA, NiNaA, CuNaA), and MFI (HZSM-5, Na-ZSM-5) were probed using $\mathrm{IR},^{2\dot{1}\dot{6}-221}\ \mathrm{UV}/\mathrm{vis},^{\dot{2}}$ 19 and nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopies.222
On aluminum-rich FAU up to $\mathrm{Si/Al}$ ratios of about 2.5 (NaX zeolites), the first $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecules are adsorbed dissociatively, producing OH groups $\left(3650~\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}\right),$ similar to those of hydrogen FAU; however, NaY-type zeolites with $\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{Al}>2.5$ adsorb the H2S molecules practically without dissociation.218 Similar conclusions were also reported by Lechert and Henning using the NMR techniqu e.222 The first molecules adsorbed on NaX dissociated completely with formation of $S^{2-}$ and $2\mathrm{OH},$ but at higher coverages, dissociation into ${\mathrm{H}}S^{-}$ and $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ (see Figure 11) as well as physical adsorption occurs.219 Four times higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption capacities for X-type zeolites than for Ytype zeolites were observed; moreover, the uptake changed in the following sequence: K-FAU $>$ Na-FAU $>$ Li-FAU, suggesting that H2S adsorption improves with FAU basicity.223 Garcia and Lercher studied adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in ZSM-5 zeolites $\mathrm{(Si/Al}=35.5\mathrm{\AA}$ ) and observed molecular adsorption on both $\mathrm{Na}-$ and H-ZSM-5 zeolite surfaces.221 In conclusion, lower $\mathrm{Si/Al}$ ratios promote dissociative adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ on cationexchanged zeolites. Recently, Sigot et al. attempted to investigate the retention mechanism of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ on $\mathrm{\DeltaNaX}$ zeolites with Si/Al ratio of $1.4.^{224}$ The authors observed poor thermal desorption at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ indicating no physisorption and the formation of stable sulfur polymers which further compromised thermal regeneration.
The SH vibration band of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorbed on NaA showed a large frequency shift; however, in the case of CaNaA, over a wide range of coverage, the characteristic frequency of the SH band remained constant at $2540~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ .217 Thus, the authors concluded that no significant sorbate−sorbate interactions occur and that all $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecules occupy equivalent sites in the case of CaNaA. No bands corresponding to adsorbed species were observed for NiNaA and CuNaA, and CoNaA adsorbed non-dissociatively, while ZnNaA and MnNaA demonstrated both dissociative and non-dissociative adsorption.220 In agreement with previous studies of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption on various zeolites,216−218 water formation was observed on adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ onto MnNaA, $\mathrm{ZnNaA},$ and CoNaA. It is not very clear, however, whether $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is formed using either framework oxygen or oxygen impurities in the gas feed. It is unclear if metal oxide clusters of the exchanged cations are present in the system and whether these form a source of oxygen for water formation. Very recently, Wynnyk and co-workers carried out precise and detailed measurements up to high pressures for adsorption of sour gas species ( $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ COS, $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ ) on zeolite 4A (LTA) with Si/Al ratios between 1.14 and 1.15 and activated under ultra-high vacuum ( $10^{-10}$ bar) up to $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ 183 The adsorption isotherms of these compounds were found to be quite different, with saturation loadings extrapolated from the fitted adsorption isotherm for $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ and COS being 9.16, 7.25, 5.87, and $3.11\ \mathrm{mmol/g},$ respectively. The low saturation capacity of COS was not expected; computational calculations for HOMO bond orientation indicated that COS should be excluded from zeolite 4A, despite the experimental results. The reason for decreasing saturation loadings for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with decreasing temperatures (0, 25, and $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ) was not explained by the authors. The authors also calculated the enthalpy of adsorption using isotherms at different temperatures; at low loadings, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and COS yield similar values of 20 $\mathrm{kJ/mol},$ , while $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ interact more strongly, with values of ${\sim}35\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ (near saturation loading). These enthalpies of adsorption seem too small to suggest a chemisorption behavior; however, it should be noted that these interactions can be sensitive to even trace amounts of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ that may accompany the feed gas.
$\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption on cluster models of metal-exchanged ZSM12 ( $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathrm{Fe},$ , Co, Ni, $\mathrm{Cu,}$ and $Z\mathbf{n}$ atoms) was investigated using DFT calculations, and the reported enthalpies of adsorption were −77, −77, −90, −58, and −76 kJ/mol, respectively.225 This suggests that Ni binds most strongly to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ , while the binding with $\mathtt{C u}$ is the weakest. One can see that the trends for different metals are not the same as reported for cationexchanged LTA zeolites described above.220 A key issue here is that we are not certain of the fraction of the cations in the ionexchanged sites versus that in the metal oxide form. Several other factors, such as difference in the confinement of LTA and MTW zeolites for the same metallic species, presence of impurities in experimental measurements, or inadequate level of theory to describe the system using DFT, can also contribute to the different trends in the two zeolite types. In the absence of more data, it is hard to draw concrete conclusions about the affinity of various transition metal cations toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ .
SAPO-43, a small-pore silicoaluminophosphate, is a microporous sorbent with appropriate dimensions for sour gas sieving; however, its low thermal stability up to about $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ prevents its utilization for adsorptive separations. Elemental analysis and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ heats of adsorption point to the presence of amine-like compounds entrapped in the surface of SAPO-43, which are believed to keep the framework from collapsing.226 Hernández-Maldonado et al. demonstrated the use of partially gas.226 Regeneration with pure hel2ium2 at calcined SAPO-43 to separate $\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ and $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was sufficient from natural to remove almost all of the adsorbed water, but only $40\%$ of the adsorption capacity was restored for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ . The authors suggest that regeneration using chemicals such as hydrogen peroxide $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ may help in entirely removing $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ without the need to thermally regenerate the adsorbent.
Crespo et al. compared the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ breakthrough capacities among $\mathrm{{Cu(I)Y},}$ , $\mathrm{Cu(II)Y,}$ CuCl/MCM-41, CuCl/SBA-15, and $\mathrm{AgY}$ .227 It was found from both ab initio molecular orbital calculations and the experimental temperature dependence of adsorption isotherms that the energy of adsorption decreased in the following order: $\mathrm{Cu(I)Y>Ag\bar{Y}>C u(I\bar{I})Y}$ . The chemical composition of $\mathrm{AgY}$ zeolites, $\mathrm{Ag}_{63}\mathrm{Al}_{57}\mathrm{Si}_{135}\mathrm{O}_{384},$ clearly shows that the $\mathrm{Ag/Al}$ ratio is greater than unity; this suggests thats some of the cations are located at sites other than the cationexchange sites. Structural characterization of cation-exchanged zeolites228 may help in better understanding some of the anomalies in trends in affinity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ toward various cations. Nearly $70\%$ regeneration of $\mathrm{{Cu}(I)Y}$ zeolite was achieved by heating at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a helium environment, and $100\%$ regeneration could be accomplished by reacting with air at 350 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ this is another indication of cations in their oxide form. With a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capacity at concentrations as low as $100\ \mathrm{ppm},$ high selectivity over $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ and being fully regenerable at a moderate temperature $(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , $\mathrm{Cu(I)Y,}$ CuCl/SBA-15, and CuCl/MCM-41 materials hold some promise for natural gas desulfurization.
In addition to the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ impurity, most natural gas fields also contain other impurities such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ CO, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}$ , and these polar molecules may have a tendency to compete with, and sometime even supersede, $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption. Kumar et al. carried out a detailed investigation of such mixture systems for Na-, $\mathrm{Ag\bar{\cdot},}$ and $\mathtt{C u}$ -exchanged FAU at high and low $\mathrm{Si/Al}$ ratios (see Figure 12).229 The authors report a nearly complete loss in $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{\DeltaNaX}$ zeolite in the presence of either $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ CO, or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , while $\mathrm{CuX}$ and $\mathrm{CuY}$ showed similar losses in selectivity in the presence of CO. $\mathrm{\bfAgX}$ and $\mathrm{AgY}$ zeolites maintained $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capacities of about $30{-}40~\mathrm{mg/g}$ at $10\ \mathrm{ppm}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{;}}$ even in the presence of all three impurities. The authors could also explain the observed experimental trends using DFT calculations; comparisons of the structures and energetics between the cluster and periodic calculations led to the conclusion that the confinement effect from the complete zeolitic framework is insignificant for the adsorbates studied. Further DFT calculations showed that, while the presence of the zeolite does not change radically the trend in the adsorption energies for alkali metal cations, in the case of transition metal cations it enhances the CO adsorption markedly relative to all other adsorbates.230 The authors also investigated other cationexchanged zeolites such as $\mathrm{Zn(II)Y,Ni(II)Y,}$ and $\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{III})\mathrm{Y}$ and found that they are all subject to strong adsorption of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .231 It should be noted here that strict ion exchange (without formation of metal oxides) has not been demonstrated for these cations.
In addition to the competitive adsorption in the presence of impurities, Bülow and co-workers reported on reactive adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ on cation-exchanged LTA and FAU zeolites.232−234 Due to strongly preferred sorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ on these zeolites $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}~\gg~\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}~\ge~\mathrm{CO}_{2}~\approx~\mathrm{COS}\right)$ , the equilibrium of the reaction $\mathrm{H}_{2}S\ +\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{COS}\ +\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is shifted to the right. As a result, one sulfur-containing species $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}S\right)$ is transformed to another (COS), with nearly zero enrichment of sulfur on the molecular sieve. The authors have extensively studied different variations of the adsorbent such as type of zeolite, $\mathrm{Si/Al}$ ratio, choice of cation for exchange, degree of ion exchange (percentage of $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ exchanged), and presorption of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ . At room temperature, $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ conversion on LTA can be as high as $50\%$ for a $\mathrm{Si/Al}$ ratio of 1, and drops to nearly zero for a $\mathrm{Si/Al}$ ratio of 3.232 At higher degrees of ion exchange, there is a considerable kinetic barrier, and one can observe $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ conversions anywhere between 20 and $75\%$ , depending on the reaction tim e.232,233 Thus, in the absence of any known method to suppress this reaction, the authors suggest short-time techniques such as PSA/VSA.234 This study suggests that future investigations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ breakthrough behavior in cationic zeolites should also monitor COS breakthrough.
3.4.3. Titanosilicates. In 1989, several synthetic titanosilicates were introduced as oxides or molecular sieves that have not only tetrahedrally coordinated sites that are characteristic of zeolites such as aluminosilicates or aluminophosphates but also octahedrally coordinated framework sites.235 These materials possess moderately high surface areas $(S_{\mathrm{BET}}\approx200\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ , and if active metals are well-dispersed on these supports, high metal utilization for reactive adsorption can be attained. Rezaei et al. have recently investigated low-temperature reactive $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal using copper-exchanged Engelhard titanosilicates (ETS-2, ETS-4, ETS-10).236 While ETS-4 and ETS-10 are microporous, ETS-2 is most probably just a layered titanate and is too dense to be considered a molecular sieve.235 $\mathrm{c}\mathrm{u}$ -ETS-2, Cu-ETS-4, and Cu-ETS-10 show $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ breakthrough capacities of 1.4, 0.32, and $1.3\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ and copper utilization of 71, 44, and $98\%$ , respectively. The high and low copper utilization in ETS-10 and ETS-4 has been attributed to their large and small pores, respectively, leading to pore blockage by adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in case of ETS-4. Since ETS-10 allows near complete utilization of copper sites at $8.4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ copper loading, higher loadings should be investigated to possibly improve breakthrough capacities. In a follow-up work, the authors investigated the effect of exchange metals such as Ag, Ca, Cu and $Z\mathrm{n}$ on ETS-2 and compared to a fully developed commercial $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorbent (R3-11G, $36~\mathrm{wt\%}$ CuO, BASF).237 The results indicated $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ uptake capacities in the following order: $\mathrm{Cu-ETS}{\cdot}2>\mathrm{Ag}$ -ETS-2 $\mathrm{>Zn{-}E T S{-}2\approx R3{-}11G>C a{-}E T S{-}2\approx N a{-}E T S{-}}$ 2. In a recent study, Roller et al. studied Cu-ETS-2, Ce-ETS-2, and Cu-CeETS-2 over a wide range of temperatures and under hydrogenrich and water-rich atmospheres and found that, contrary to Ce-modified sorbents in the literature,153 Ce-based ETS-2 showed a very low $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ breakthrough capacity.238
## 3.5. Carbon-Based Adsorbents
3.5.1. Activated Carbons. Activated carbon (AC) with surface areas easily in excess of $1000\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ due to a high degree of microporosity is a well-known material for adsorption and catalysis. It is most commonly manufactured by carbonizing easily available carbon sources such as coconut shell, wood, coal, etc. at high temperatures in an inert atmosphere. The resultant coke has pores too small for use as an adsorbent, and usually steam activation is carried out to enlarge the pore structure, making it accessible for adsorption. Several studies have been reported in the literature on the use of ACs for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption as well as Claus reaction between $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to form elemental sulfur. Unlike crystalline materials such as MOFs and zeolites, ACs are amorphous and hence characterized using ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption, $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ measurements, thermal analysis, ion chromatography, elemental analysis, and FTIR techniques. It is nearly impossible to rationalize the large amounts of data in this field with various carbon sources, activation conditions, $\mathrm{\pH}$ of adsorption, and presence of unidentifiable impurities in the ACs. An attempt is made to highlight some of the studies that show high adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{,}}$ , but the reasons for most of these are not well-understood.
Turk et al. have demonstrated the injection of basic chemicals such as ammonia and caustic solutions to enhance $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption.239,240 There are numerous investigations from the Bandosz group on removal of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ using ACs that differ in their surface areas, pore volumes, and surface acidities.241−246 The heat of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption on a variety of ACs, differing in surface chemistry, $\mathrm{\tt{pH}},$ , or the presence of specific oxygenated groups, was shown to be between 39 and $47~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , about 2 times that obtained for a nonporous surface.242 Adib et al. tried to increase the basicity of AC by impregnating it with urea; however, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ conversion decreased by about $10\%$ from 298 to $272~\mathrm{mg/g}$ for the impregnated samples compared to the initial sample, possibly due to an $18\%$ decrease in surface area on urea impregnation.244 Tian et al. chemically modified the surface of AC using 3-aminopropyltriethoxysilane (APTES), thus imparting a basic character to the sorbent with the goal of enhancing interaction with acidic $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ .247 The authors observed poor breakthrough capacities of ${<}1\ \mathrm{mg/g}$ at $1600~\mathrm{ppm}$ , maybe due to blockage of pores because of the bulky APTES impregnation. At $8200\mathrm{ppm}$ the equilibrium adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is 18.13, 8.88, and $6.03~\mathrm{mg/g}$ for APTES-AC, $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}-$ AC, and AC, respectively. Moisture and temperature were found to have adverse effects on $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal, while oxygen favored $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal. The negative role of humidity in this study is contradictory to previous studies of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption on ${\mathrm{AC}},^{241}$ while the positive effect of oxygen has b2een explained by the authors to be due to the catalytic role of oxygen during oxidation.
Regeneration of ACs using cold and hot water washing and using heating at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air atmosphere showed that, after the first adsorption run, the capacity of carbon for hydrogen sulfide adsorption significantly decreased (around $60\%$ ) due to irreversible adsorption in the most active adsorption cites located in small pores; subsequent runs revealed a more or less constant capacity.248,249 Boudou et al. could regenerate the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ -adsorbed AC sorbents by heating for $10\mathrm{min}$ in flowing $\Nu_{2}$ at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .250 Although inert atmosphere can be maintained at laboratory scales, the possibility of self-ignition at such high temperatures of regeneration cannot be eliminated. Aging of ACs has shown that the sorbents having significant capacity after the first adsorption run are practically unwashable with water due to the deposition of bulky sulfur polymers that are resistant to oxidation.251
3.5.2. Structured Carbons. Structured nanoporous carbon materials may be broadly classified into two main kinds: graphene-based materials and carbon nanotubes (CNTs). Graphene is an allotrope of carbon that consists of a twodimensional hexagonal lattice of single atomic layer of $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ - hybridized carbon atoms. Graphene-based materials may be further classified as pristine graphene, graphene oxide (GO), and reduced graphene oxide (rGO).252 CNTs are cylindrical nanostructures that may be imagined as seamlessly rolled-up graphene sheets with channel diameters as small as $0.4~\mathrm{nm},^{2\hat{5}3}$ ${\sim}1.4~\mathrm{nm}$ in several studies,254 and can be as high as $50~\mathrm{nm}$ .252 The CNTs can be open or closed with half-fullerene caps at either ends of the cylindrical structure. These single-walled CNTs (SWCNTs) are usually observed as closely packed bundles on a honeycomb lattice with typically 100 nanotubes (of $1.4\ \mathrm{nm}$ diameter) forming bundles of $<20~\mathrm{nm}$ diameter.255 The adsorption sites in SWCNT bundles maybe classified into three kinds: inside each tube, interstitial channels between the tubes, and outer surface peripheral to the bundle. The surface area for these materials may range from over $2500~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for a
SWCNT to as low as $800~\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for bundles of infinite diameter.255 SWCNTs of varying channel diameters can also arrange in the form of several concentric cylinders to form multi-walled nanotubes (MWNTs).
Several groups have theoretically investigated the role of defects in the adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ on the graphene surface, mainly for sensor applications.256−259 Depending on the position of binding, adsorption energies of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ on pristine graphene were found to be between 10 and $16\mathrm{~kJ/mol}.$ , but having a defect site with the sulfur atom of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ pointing toward the defect resulted in a much stronger binding $(88~\mathrm{kJ/mol}),$ .256 In another study, three different graphene structures (pristine, with divacancy, and with the inclusion of nitrogen in graphene) were investigated, and the binding energies were found to be 1.6, 1.3, and $2.5~\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ , respectively.257 Borisova et al. investigated the effect of vacancy in graphene and found that $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorbed by forming weak $s{\mathrm{-C}}$ covalent bonds with the atoms surrounding the single vacancy.258 In addition to the single vacancy and divacancies in graphene described above, there can be several other defect types; one such defect is the Stone−Wales (SW) defect that has four hexagons fused into two alternating pentagons and heptagons. Hegde et al. studied $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ binding to the carbon site of pristine graphene, SW defect site, and BN sites of BN-doped graphene through firstprinciples DFT with van der Waals (vdW) interactions.259 While the adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is weak at both C and BN sites, with a binding energy of $15\mathrm{kJ/mol},$ it is significantly stronger at the SW defect site with a binding energy of $26~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ In the relaxed geometries, the sulfur atom of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ points toward graphene (at a distance $3.14\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) at $\mathrm{~\cal~C~}$ and BN sites, while the molecule’s H atoms point toward graphene (at a distance 2.84 Å) at the SW defect site. There seem to be significant differences in the various predictions for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption on graphene; these differences may be due to different levels of theories employed for the independent studies. A consistent treatment of all defect types will be important in order to understand, quantitatively, the role of graphene defects in $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption.
There are several studies employing DFT for adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ on metal-doped graphene .256,260−265 Strong adsorption was found due to a large binding energy $(615\mathrm{~kJ/mol}$ for 1 $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{Pt}$ system and $75\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ for the $\mathrm{7~H_{2}S/P t}$ system) and short $\mathrm{Pt}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ bond length compared to those of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}/$ graphene system.260 Zhang et al. reported binding energies of 16, 64, 174, and $185\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ for pristine, Ca-, Co-, and Fedoped graphene, respectively.256 Sharma and Verma calculated binding energies of 33, 42, 123, and $98~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ for pristine, $\mathrm{B}_{\widetilde{,}}$ Al-, and Ga-doped graphene, respectively.261 Pang et al. reported binding energies of 15, 81, 102, and $112\mathrm{~kJ/mol}$ for pristine, Cr-, Cu-, and W-doped graphene, respectively.265 These highly energetic interactions of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ with metal-doped graphene are mainly of interest for sensor applications; however, they may have potential usefulness in adsorbents for ultra-deep sulfur removal of natural gas as the final polishing step for direct-methane fuel cells.170
The effect of the presence of S atom on graphene on the adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ was investigated using DFT.266 Two types of systems were investigated: S-functionalized graphene, where an S atom was added on pristine graphene, and S-doped graphene, where an S atom was added to the single-vacancy defect of graphene. Pristine graphene showed adsorption energies of 34 $\mathrm{kJ/mol},$ , while S-functionalized graphene showed energies as high as $170\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ due to covalent bond formation between S and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ for configurations with H atoms pointing away from the graphene plane. Both physisorption and chemisorption were observed, depending upon $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}-S$ bond distances in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S/S$ -doped graphene complex before optimization.
Molecular simulations for adsorption on structured carbons in the literature give very inconsistent answers and use idealized structures that may not be experimentally realizable.267−270 Neglect of electrostatic interactions for the adsorbent in studies involving polar sorbate molecules may result in incorrect predictions of sorbate−sorbent interactions. The potential of GO has not yet been extensively explored by molecular modeling, mainly due to the lack of an atomistic GO model for theoretical calculations.271 Huang et al. have reported the controllable generation of atomistic GO models by a temperature-programmed reactive MD simulation. They further calculated the reactive adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ mixtures on the as-generated atomistic GO structures.271 $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ was observed to dissociate on the carbonyl functional groups, in agreement with the dynamic adsorption experiments; for the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ mixtures, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules were preferentially adsorbed to the carbonyl sites, thus blocking the availability of active sites for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ decomposition, again in agreement with the experimental findings.
Mohamadalizadeh et al. investigated the sorption of pure $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ on AC, MWNTs, CNTs decorated with tungsten nanoparticles (W-CNTs), and amino-functionalized CNTs (F-CNTs).272 Although these CNT-based materials have a surface area $(\sim100$ $\mathbf{m}^{2}/\mathbf{g})$ lower than that of AC $(\sim1000~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ , the saturation capacities for these systems (at 10 bar and $20{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{}^{\cdot}$ ) did not differ drastically, and CNTs showed better regenerability compared to AC.
## 3.6. Composite Materials
3.6.1. Metal Oxides/Mesoporous Silica. While metal oxide sorbents show high affinity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ , they suffer from limitations such as loss in surface area and porosity, and this subsequently results in degraded performance during cyclic sulfidation and regeneration. Ordered mesoporous molecular sieves that allow narrow pore sizes (16−100 Å) tailored through the choice of surfactant, auxiliary chemicals, and reaction conditions were first discovered in the early 1990s.273,274 This seminal discovery enabling uniform pore sizes of mesoporous materials with high surface areas opened up a new area for novel heterogeneous catalysts.
Wang et al. for the first time investigated adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ on metal-oxide-impregnated mesoporous silica, ZnO/SBA$15^{275}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{\bar{S}B\bar{A}}{-}15$ .276 The breakthrough capacities mentioned by those authors are not in accord with the breakthrough times they report; using the details of the experimental setup, we calculated new breakthrough capacities which are reported in Table 9. In an inert medium, $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ reacts chemically with metal oxides, and hence, at most the adsorbed amount can correspond to $100\%$ utilization of the metal oxide on the high-surface-area substrates. However, these two studies show $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption far exceeding the theoretical maximum, suggesting that Claus reaction occurs between $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from the air in the feed and the metal oxide functions as catalyst for this reaction rather that itself reacting with $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ to form metal sulfide.
Dhage et al. studied room-temperature $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption on transition-metal-promoted $\mathrm{{ZnO}}$ , impregnated on high-surfacearea $({\sim}550~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\dot{/}}\mathrm{g})$ $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ .277 The $\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ system showed much higher absolute breakthrough capacities as well as percentage $Z\mathbf{n}$ utilization compared to their unsupported commercial counterparts from BASF and Sud-Chemie (see Table 9). It was observed that the sulfur capacity oscillated, reproducibly, with the number of cycles rather than monotonically increasing or decreasing. Using dopants such as Mn, Fe, Co, $\mathrm{Ni,}$ and Cu improved the utilization of $\mathrm{{znO}}$ , with the effect from copper being most prominent, changing the $\scriptstyle z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ utilization at saturation from an already high $64\%$ to about $93\%$ . This increase does not seem to improve with the amount of copper, since the saturation capacities remained nearly unchanged upon increasing the $C u{:}Z_{\mathrm{n}}$ ratio from 0.05 to 0.25 at the same overall metal oxide loading. DFT studies showed that there is a significant energetic discrimination between $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ binding to a ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\breve{2}+}$ versus a $Z\bar{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ site; this difference is smaller for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{\breve{O}}$ .278 On a $\mathrm{{znO}}$ surface with some $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ sites substituted with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , there can be a displacement of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ at the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ surface sites. This is yet another explanation for the promoter effect by copper, in addition to several other arguments such as surface area and diffusion invoked earlier. Yang and Tatarchuk279 also carried out similar studies and found significantly enhanced utilization by doping $\mathrm{znO}$ with copper or silver. In another study, they studied Fe- and Mnpromoted $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ sorbents supported on silica and found higher metal utilization compared to undoped $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ ; however, no significant preference was observed for Fe or Mn.280 For the first time, real-time in situ electron spin resonance (ESR) spectroscopy was performed during reactive adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ . It was inferred that the surface of the sorbents is enriched with Fe promoter cations, while the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{3+}$ promoter cations are located within the supported $\mathrm{{znO}}$ nanocrystallites. In situ experiments over multiple cycles may help to further elucidate the mechanism behind the oscillatory behavior.
Hussain et al. investigated the effect of different supports for $\mathrm{ZnO}_{\mathrm{i}}^{\cdot}$ ; SBA-15 allows much higher breakthrough capacities as well as $\mathrm{{ZnO}}$ utilization.281 This further highlights the importance of designing increasingly improved supports for metal oxides and suggests that $\mathrm{{Cu-Zn}}$ mixed oxides doped on SBA-15 may demonstrate a high capacity. In addition to capacity, stable performance of the adsorbent over several regeneration cycles is an important factor that may impact the economic viability of the process; in general, it is hard to reproducibly achieve the capacity of fresh adsorbents.284−286 Generally, nanoparticles exhibit a tendency to grow into larger crystallites during the process of high-temperature regeneration. Inspired by the work of Prieto et al., who developed a highly stable methanol synthesis catalyst of $\mathrm{{Cu-ZnO}}$ nanoparticles on SBA-15,288 Elyassi et al. investigated this adsorbent for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal, resulting in extremely high and fully regenerable breakthrough capacities and ZnO utilization over five regeneration cycles.282
The major drawback of such composite sorbents is that the active component forms only a small fraction of the overall mass of the material, so even though high metal oxide utilizations can be achieved, the absolute amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ that can be removed per unit mass of the adsorbent remains low. Attempts to increase the loading of metal oxide have not succeeded in increasing the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capacity without loss in $\%$ utilization.283,284,287 Future research to increase the fraction of the active material in the composite with high utilization of the active component and high regenerability of the sorbents will help develop even better materials.
3.6.2. Supported Amines. Supported amine adsorbents can be broadly classified into three classes: Class 1 constitutes adsorbents with physically loaded amine species, Class 2 adsorbents contain a covalent link between the amine and the solid support, mostly via silane chemistry or via polymeric supports with amine-containing side chains, and Class 3 adsorbents comprise porous supports upon which aminopolymers are polymerized in situ, starting from an aminecontaining monome r.289 Class 3 materials combine the higher amine loadings of class 1 materials and the enhanced stability of class 2 materials due to amine−support covalent linkages.290,291
Huang et al. first reported on $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal using supported amines by employing high-surface-area silica supports (silica xerogel $(\dot{8}16\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\dot{\bf g})$ and MCM-48 $\left(1389\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\right)$ ) functionalized with with 3-aminopropyl $\mathrm{(-CH_{2}C H_{2}C H_{2}N H_{2})}$ groups.292 Adsorption/desorption was measured for pure-component $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{.}}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ with and without moisture. At atmospheric pressure and temperature, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ /amino group ratio was observed to be 0.7 and 0.9 for xerogel and MCM-48, respectively. These ratios are 0.08 and 0.05 for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and 0.29 and 0.25 for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ at the highest measurement pressure of 0.001 atm $\left(1000~\mathrm{ppmv}\right)$ for xerogel and MCM-48, respectively. The sorbents could be almost completely regenerated by pressure swing or temperature swing at $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The results indicated that the presence of water vapor doubled the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed and barely affected the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption.
Zhou et al. explored Class 1 adsorbents by physically loading triethanolamine (C6H15O3N; TEA) on silica gel (335 m2/g).293 Due to the weaker basicity of TEA, its sorption capacity is nearly 100 times less than that of amine-modified silicas.292 The breakthrough capacity doubled on loading the silica gel with 20 ${\bf w t\%}$ of TEA and increased 5-fold when the loading was increased to a mere $27.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ . There is no clear explanation for this sudden rise in adsorption, except that small selective pores that are otherwise unexposed to TEA may get filled with TEA at a higher loading of $27.5\mathrm{\wt\%}$ . In a subsequent study, the authors used this system for studying a multi-cycle pressure swing adsorption setup.294 In a follow-up work, the authors synthesized SBA-15 as a high specific surface area TEA carrier and reported that the amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorbed on the TEAmodified SBA-15 is 1.85 times more than that on the TEAmodified silica gel.295 Xue and Liu tried another tertiary amine, methyl-diethyl-amine, loaded on SBA-15, and found an optimal amine loading of $60~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for an $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ breakthrough capacity of $0.1\ \mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g}.\dot{}$ 96 Although the adsorbed amine is stated to be methyldiethylamine, such low loadings, comparable to those for TEA, are more representative of methyldiethanolamine.
In another study, physically loaded polyethylenimine (PEI) particles on mesoporous molecular sieves MCM-41 at $50\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ loading were explored for low-temperature $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 10 ppmv inlet concentration.297 The adsorbent could maintain an outlet concentration of $0.04\mathrm{\ppmv}$ . The utility of these sorbents for natural gas sweetening at higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentrations needs to be investigated. In yet another study, linear PEI was dispersed inside the meso-channels of a SBA-15 framework, allowing interaction of amine groups in PEI with silanol groups on SBA-15.298 The breakthrough and saturation capacities increased with inlet $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentrations of 2000, 4000, and 6000 ppmv in the ranges 0.68, 1.18, 1.48 and 1.50, 2.06, $2.38\ \mathrm{mmol/g},$ respectively. The saturation value for amine-grafted sorbents at $1000\ \mathrm{ppmv}$ is $0.56\mathrm{\mmol/g.}^{292}$ So unlike TEA, PEI-modified supports show higher affinity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S},$ probably due to higher basicity of PEI, but possibly due to higher swelling of PEI compared to TEA. This is an open question that can be addressed perhaps by molecular simulations. The presence of moisture proved to enhance the adsorption capacity since only one amine group is needed per $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecule in the presence of moisture, versus two in its absence. However, moisture also resulted in a decrease in $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ desorption rates. The same authors also studied the effects of the operating temperature $\left(22-75\mathrm{~~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)$ , gas hourly space velocity (GHSV; $3\bar{3}7{-}1011~\mathrm{h^{-1}}$ ), amount of PEI loading, and different molecular sieve supports (MCM-41, MCM-48, and SBA-15).299 The structure of the mesoporous molecular sieves influences the kinetic capacity of the supported PEI sorbents; large pore size and three-dimensional channel structure favor higher breakthrough capacity. A decrease in temperature favors both breakthrough and saturation capacities, while a decrease in GHSV enhances the breakthrough capacity. The amount of PEI loading has a strong synergetic influence on the sorption performance: 50 wt% PEI on SBA-15 gives the best breakthrough capacity, while $65\mathrm{\wt\%}$ PEI on SBA-15 shows the highest saturation capacity. The developed sorbents exhibit a breakthrough capacity as high as $0.{\bar{7}}9\ \mathrm{\mmol/g}$ and a saturation capacity of $3.02\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $22~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using $4000~\mathrm{ppm}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{.}}$ , $20\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in bulk ${\bf N}_{2}$ . In addition, the developed sorbents can be regenerated easily at mild conditions $(75-100^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ , with sorption capacity maintained over eight cycles.
The studies discussed so far did not involve $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption in the presence of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ which is known to be much more acidic compared to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ . Ma et al. have made significant progress by finding an exceptional dependence of these sorbents on temperature for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ competitive sorption and developing an innovative sorption process.300 These PEI/ SBA-15 sorbents show an exceptionally high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ loading at 75 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\left(3.2\mathrm{\mmol/g}\right.$ at $0.15\ \mathrm{atm}\rangle$ ), but the lower kinetic barrier for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ compared to $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ which may be attributed to the faster proton-transfer reaction mechanism in the case of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ results in higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ breakthrough capacities at a lower temperature of $22^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The authors proposed a two-stage process, with the first column being operated at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ removal and the second column at $22~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal (see Figure 13).
Yoosuk and co-workers carried out similar two-stage studies for PEI loaded on low-cost fumed silica (fSi) and also investigated regeneration studies over multiple cycles.301 Although the authors observed high breakthrough capacities for both $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ for pure-component adsorption at 80 and $30\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively, in the mixture adsorption with two-column configuration, the working capacity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was enhanced from 2.2 to about $3.5\mathrm{\mmol/g},$ while that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ decreased from the expected 1.2 to about $0.05\mathrm{\mmol/g}$ . This reduced performance for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ compared to the system introduced by Ma et al. can be attributed to two factors: (i) the difference in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ pure-component adsorption at $22~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for PEI/ SBI-15 is much higher compared to the difference at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for PEI/fSi, and (ii) the mixture feed contained a very high concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(20\%)$ compared to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ $(0.36\%)$ in the work of Yoosuk et al.; these values were $2.4\%$ and $0.40\%$ , respectively, for the work of Ma et al. These differences demonstrate the importance of choice of process conditions in selecting optimal sorbents. Similar to the discovery of novel zeolites for natural gas purification through a combined material screening and process optimization approach,302 process optimization in materials selection for supported amines can help discover optimal sorbents.
In spite of these exciting properties, the powder nature of ordered mesoporous silica inhibits its direct usage in industrial packed columns due to very high pressure drops. Chen et al. investigated hierarchical porous silica supports for PEI. They can be easily formed as monoliths, which can be directly used in the fixed bed without any shaping process.303 The largest breakthrough capacity of these sorbents was $1.27\mathrm{\mmol/g},$ about $60\%$ higher than that of PEI/SBA-15 $\left(0.79\mathrm{\mmol/g}\right)$ or MCM-41 $(0.81\mathrm{\mmol/g})$ , although the test for the former was carried out at a lower inlet $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentration and a higher GHSV.299 A new parameter, polymerization degrees of PEI $(423,600,15000,750000)$ , was investigated, and it was found that the sorbent with a PEI molecular weight of 600 had the best sorption property. The authors propose that PEI with higher polymerization degree should have very large steric hindrance and low fluidity, restraining the internal diffusion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and lowering the amine utilization, while PEI (423), a mixture of linear monomers and branch polymers, may be partially consumed or volatilized during the impregnation and drying, resulting in the decrease of efficient amine groups.
Belmabkhout et al. studied triamine-grafted $\mathrm{(-CH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{-}$ $\mathrm{CH_{2}N H C H_{2}C H_{2}N H C H_{2}C H_{2}N H_{2}})$ pore-expanded mesoporous silica (TRI-PE-MCM-41) for adsorption of dry $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and found a much higher affinity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ compared to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (see Figure 14).304 Binary $S_{\mathrm{H}_{2}S/C O_{2}}$ varied from 70 to 10 upon varying the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentrations from 5 to $100\ \mathrm{\ppm}.$ 305 These materials thus provide a promising solution to single-step $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ removal. Such simultaneous removal is desirable if re-injection of concentrated sour gas stream into empty gas fields is preferred over conversion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ to elemental sulfur by the Claus process. $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption isotherms in the temperature range of $25\mathrm{-}80$ $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are available from two different studies, and both predict an isosteric heat of adsorption between $-40$ and $-20\mathrm{~kJ/mol}$ with increasing loading; the low-loading heats of adsorption can be as high as −100 kJ/mol.305,306
Another Class 1 adsorbent study employed SBA-15 loaded with hexamethylenetetraamine for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal from a stream containing $9000\ p\mathrm{{pm}\ H_{2}\cal{S}}$ in $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ ; the highest loading was observed for $50\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ amine loading with a capacity of 0.75 $\mathrm{mmol/g},$ stable over 10 cycles when overnight regeneration was conducted using $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .307 In general, all sorbents in this category can be fully regenerated under mild temperature conditions and are shown to maintain a constant capacity for several cycles in most of the studies. Efforts toward reducing the regeneration time will increase the productivity of these sorbents.
3.6.3. Zeolite/Polymer. Bhandari et al. developed hollow fibers with polymer “binder”, impregnated with high loadings of sulfur-selective zeolite sorbent “fillers” (see Figure 15).308
Cellulose acetate formed the binder and zeolite NaY formed the filler for these hollow fibers with high sorbent loadings and good strength. Although the hollow fiber morphology is representative of contact in a membrane process, it should be noted that the fibers here are used as adsorbents. The fiber− sorbent morphology was characterized using equilibrium sorption, gas permeation, SEM, and EDX. These sorbents were shown to be very effective for low-concentration (30 ppm) $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal, especially when compared to a packed-bed process, and were found to be fully regenerable by application of heat ( $\mathrm{~\i20~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ ).
3.6.4. Metal−Organic Framework/Graphite Oxide. It was envisioned that the GO and MOF composites would increase the dispersive forces in a highly porous MOF structure via dense nanoporous GO layers $(5{-}46~\mathrm{\textmu}96)$ .186 Petit and coworkers studied GO composites with HKUST-1 and found a synergistic effect on sorption capacity ${\bf\dot{9}}{\bf m g}/{\bf g}$ for GO, $92~\mathrm{mg/}$ $\mathbf{g}$ for HKUST-1, and $199\mathrm{mg/g}$ for composite with $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ GO). The breakthrough capacities measured at room temperature using $1000\ \mathrm{ppm\H}_{2}S$ in moist air were not found to correlate with the measured porosity of the composites, suggesting a mechanism in addition to physisorption contributing to the overall sorption. Indeed the adsorbent bed changed color from light blue (hydrated copper) to black $\mathrm{{(CuS)}}$ . Ebrahim et al. found that adsorption capacities increased by $85\%$ for composites with sulfur-doped graphite due to the presence of moisture compared to dry conditions.309 Those authors suggested that, under moist conditions, water acts as a “screening” factor, blocking the direct attack of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}/{\mathrm{SO}}_{2}$ on the copper centers and preserving the structure to some degree. They found that the catalytic effect of the modified graphene phase results in the formation of $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with a much higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ breakthrough capacity of $241\pm6~\mathrm{mg/g}$ .
In another study, Huang et al. prepared composites using MOF-5 and GO in the presence of glucose.310 GO enhanced the dispersive forces in MOF-5, as demonstrated by higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ uptake, but beyond the optimal loading of $5.25\%$ , GO led to collapse of the MOF crystal structure. At $5.25\%$ GO loading, composites with and without glucose have surface areas of 1062 and $295~\mathrm{{\m}}^{2}/\mathrm{{g}},$ respectively; the respective breakthrough capacities of these materials using 100 ppm $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ are 130.1 and $31.9\ \mathrm{mg/g}$ . The authors suggest that glucose can restrain the distortion of the composite material due to polymerization of glucose under hydrothermal conditions; these short polymers fill in the structure to support the framework, and some may even react with the functional groups on the GO (see Figure 16). While this approach provides a route to stabilize such composite materials, performance and structural reversibility of these sorbents on regeneration need to be investigated.
3.6.5. Metal (Hydr)oxide/Graphite Oxide. Several studies report on the Claus reaction between $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and air on composites of metal (hydr)oxide and GO,312−323 with breakthrough $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capacities as high as $150~\mathrm{mg/g}$ of adsorbent at 1000 ppm $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in air. Lonkar et al. demonstrated an easy in situ fabrication of nanostructured graphene/ $\mathrm{CuO}^{324}$ and graphene $\mathsf{\Delta}^{\mathrm{ZnO}^{311}}$ composites without the use of any solvents, toxic-reducing agents, or organic modifiers. The metal oxide particles in the presence of graphene had a size of sub- $20\ \mathrm{nm},$ compared to metal oxide particles of ${\sim}50~\mathrm{nm}$ synthesized by the same method (see Figure 17). At ambient temperature and with a feed composition of $0.15\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ , $0.41\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $99.4\%$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ a $50\mathrm{\mt}\%$ composite material led to over twice the capacity of pristine $\mathrm{znO}$ (see Table 10). Regeneration of these high-capacity materials with the advantage of easy synthesis is an important aspect that should be considered in the future.
3.6.6. Metal Oxide/Activated Carbon. Fauteux-Lefebvre et al. embedded iron nanoparticles in carbon nanofilaments and tested these for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ removal under a wide range of operating conditions.325 Fe K-edge spectra showed that iron was in its oxidized states ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ and $\bar{\mathrm{Fe}}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ . Even at higher temperature of $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , low breakthrough capacities were observed. Higher metal loadings of $28~\mathrm{wt\%}$ could not improve the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ uptake (see Table 11). Zhang and co-workers studied various loadings of $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ on AC under a wide range of synthesis and operating conditions and observed capacities as high as $110\mathrm{mg/g}$ for a 50 ${\bf w t\%}$ $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ loading at only $20\mathrm{\ppm\H_{2}S}.$ 326 In another study, sorbents were prepared by finely dispersing mixed $\mathrm{{znO}}$ and $\mathtt{C u O}$ onto a commercial AC at fixed total metal loading (10 wt $\%$ ) and $C u{:}Z_{\mathrm{n}}$ atomic ratios ranging from 0:1 to $1{:}1.^{\setminus32^{\div}7}$ The surface area was hardly affected due to the high dispersion of the metal oxides; moderately high $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capacities could be achieved. Only $18-27\%$ of the capacity could be regenerated at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and even at a temperature of $620\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ only $40-46\%$ of the capacity could be regenerated.
Complete regeneration and reusability of an adsorbent may or may not be feasible; therefore, it is important to consider the eventual disposal of spent adsorbents. This becomes particularly important for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ -removal applications, since contact of an improperly disposed adsorbent with air or water may result in a concentrated release into the environment and can have very serious health implications. Some of the spent metal oxides, such as iron sulfide, can be pyrophoric and can release acid gases into the atmosphere. Dissolution of these gases in water bodies can have unacceptable effects on the ecosystem. Some of the methods for treating spent adsorbents include regeneration followed by inert purge, flooding with liquid water, and steam purging.328 The cost of an adequate spentadsorbent treatment method may have large bearings on this additional cost.
## 4. MEMBRANE SEPARATION
As natural gas consumption is expected to nearly double in the coming 25 years and the raw natural gas has a varying composition depending on its origin, there is an opportunity for membrane technology to remove impurities including water, carbon dioxide, nitrogen, hydrogen sulfides, and other hydrocarbons.329 Bhide and Stern investigated the relative economics of cellulose acetate (CA) membrane technology and gas absorption using DEA and found that the membrane process for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ removal is more economical to meet the $2\%$ specification over the entire range of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ feed concentrations $(>5\%)$ .330 Presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in the feed stream resulted in an increased separation cost for meeting the specifications of both $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (4 ppm and $2\%$ , respectively). Unlike the superior performance of membranes over the entire range of acid gas concentration for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ alone, the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ allowed membranes to be economical only at higher total acid gas $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\ +\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ concentrations. While this analysis was carried out for CA membranes with $S_{\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}}=21$ and $S_{\mathrm{H}_{2}S/C H_{4}}$ $=50,^{330}$ it should be noted that the $S_{\mathrm{H}_{2}S/C\mathrm{H}_{4}}$ values for such membranes range between only 15 and 30. Membranes with higher selectivities and permeabilities than the CA membranes employed in this economic analysis may significantly expand
